[[:db/cas 17592186083629 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $csc$ is the reciprocal of $sin.$ So\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡C=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡C}.\n$$\n\nWe have that $m∠C={45}^{∘},$ which is a special angle, and its sine has the value\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡C=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡C & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡C} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that $\\csc $ is the reciprocal of $sin.$ So\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡C=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡C}.\n$$\n\nWe have that $m∠C={45}^{∘},$ which is a special angle, and its sine has the value\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡C=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡C & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡C} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186088254 :question/worked-solution "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny≈1.946\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "First, we take $\\ln $ of both sides of the equation and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y} & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\ny≈1.946\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186088264 :question/worked-solution "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-2x} & =5 \\\\ \\ln ⁡({e}^{1-2x}) & =\\ln ⁡(5) \\\\ 1-2x & =\\ln ⁡(5) \\\\ -2x & =\\ln ⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-\\ln ⁡(5)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈-0.305\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." "First, we take $\\ln $ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-2x} & =5 \\\\ \\ln ⁡({e}^{1-2x}) & =\\ln ⁡(5) \\\\ 1-2x & =\\ln ⁡(5) \\\\ -2x & =\\ln ⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1-\\ln ⁡(5)}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx≈-0.305\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186143185 :question/problem "$9hr =$ {{field-1}}\n$min$" "$9hr =$ {{field-1}}\n$\\min $"] [:db/cas 17592186147344 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)+3{sin}^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′} &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(3x)+{cos}^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x) = 6\\cos x.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos(3x), \\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions $y_1$ and $y_2$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))^{′}-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))^{′}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)) \\\\  & =3{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3x)+3{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))}^{′} &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) &=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for $u_2.$ We start by eliminating $u_1'$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\nu_1' = -\\dfrac{u_2'\\sin(3x)}{\\cos(3x)}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ -3\\left(-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}+3{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3x)+{\\cos }^{2}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 3{u}_{2}^{′}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x) = 2\\cos x \\cos(3x).$"] [:db/cas 17592186149413 :answer/value "mod" "\\bmod "] [:db/cas 17592186149474 :answer/value "8(mod9)" "8(\\bmod 9)"] [:db/cas 17592186149475 :answer/value "7(mod9)" "7(\\bmod 9)"] [:db/cas 17592186149476 :answer/value "4(mod9)" "4(\\bmod 9)"] [:db/cas 17592186149477 :answer/value "6(mod9)" "6(\\bmod 9)"] [:db/cas 17592186149478 :answer/value "3(mod9)" "3(\\bmod 9)"] [:db/cas 17592186149480 :answer/value "2(mod5)" "2(\\bmod 5)"] [:db/cas 17592186149481 :answer/value "0(mod5)" "0(\\bmod 5)"] [:db/cas 17592186149482 :answer/value "3(mod5)" "3(\\bmod 5)"] [:db/cas 17592186149483 :answer/value "4(mod5)" "4(\\bmod 5)"] [:db/cas 17592186149484 :answer/value "1(mod5)" "1(\\bmod 5)"] [:db/cas 17592186149536 :answer/value "mod" "\\bmod "] [:db/cas 17592186151002 :answer/value "2(mod7)" "2(\\bmod 7)"] [:db/cas 17592186151003 :answer/value "1(mod7)" "1(\\bmod 7)"] [:db/cas 17592186151004 :answer/value "3(mod7)" "3(\\bmod 7)"] [:db/cas 17592186151005 :answer/value "4(mod7)" "4(\\bmod 7)"] [:db/cas 17592186151006 :answer/value "5(mod7)" "5(\\bmod 7)"] [:db/cas 17592186151194 :answer/value "$x≡2, 5$ only $(mod8)$" "$x≡2, 5$ only $(\\bmod 8)$"] [:db/cas 17592186151195 :answer/value "$x≡1, 4$ only $(mod8)$" "$x≡1, 4$ only $(\\bmod 8)$"] [:db/cas 17592186151196 :answer/value "$x≡3, 5$ only $(mod8)$" "$x≡3, 5$ only $(\\bmod 8)$"] [:db/cas 17592186151197 :answer/value "$x≡2, 4$ only $(mod8)$" "$x≡2, 4$ only $(\\bmod 8)$"] [:db/cas 17592186151201 :answer/value "$x≡1, 4, 7$ only $(mod9)$" "$x≡1, 4, 7$ only $(\\bmod 9)$"] [:db/cas 17592186151202 :answer/value "$x≡0, 2, 4, 8$ only $(mod9)$" "$x≡0, 2, 4, 8$ only $(\\bmod 9)$"] [:db/cas 17592186151203 :answer/value "$x≡1, 4$ only $(mod9)$" "$x≡1, 4$ only $(\\bmod 9)$"] [:db/cas 17592186151204 :answer/value "$x≡1, 3, 5$ only $(mod9)$" "$x≡1, 3, 5$ only $(\\bmod 9)$"] [:db/cas 17592186151853 :question/worked-solution "Since the modulus $n=6$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx = 0,1,2,3,4,5\n$$\n\ninto the equation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 0 & ≡0≢4 & (mod6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 1 & ≡2≢4 & (mod6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2 & ≡4 & (mod6) & ✓ \\\\ 2 \\cdot 3 & ≡0≢4 & (mod6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 4 & ≡2≢4 & (mod6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5 & ≡4 & (mod6) & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x\\equiv 2, 5$ only $(\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$." "Since the modulus $n=6$ is fairly small, we can solve the congruence by trial and error.\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\nx = 0,1,2,3,4,5\n$$\n\ninto the equation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\cdot 0 & ≡0≢4 & (\\bmod 6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 1 & ≡2≢4 & (\\bmod 6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2 & ≡4 & (\\bmod 6) & ✓ \\\\ 2 \\cdot 3 & ≡0≢4 & (\\bmod 6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 4 & ≡2≢4 & (\\bmod 6) & \\cdot \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5 & ≡4 & (\\bmod 6) & ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x\\equiv 2, 5$ only $(\\textrm{mod}\\:6)$."] [:db/cas 17592186151855 :question/worked-solution "In the given congruence, we have $a=8$ and $n=20$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,20)=4\n$$\n\nSince $b=4$ is divisible by $4$, our congruence has $4$ solutions.\n\nFirst, we reduce the congruence using the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle 8 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  4 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 20 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 4 \\cdot  2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  4 \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 4 \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{4} \\cdot  2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\cancel{4} \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} \\cancel{4} \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  1 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the new congruence, we have $a_1=2$ and $n_1=5$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=\\text{gcd}(2,5)=1\n$$\n\nThis means our congruence has a unique solution modulo $5$.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$. To find the inverse of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$, we compute the exponents of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2^{2} & ≡4 & (mod5) \\\\ 2^{3} & ≡4 \\cdot 2≡8≡1 \\cdot 5 + 3≡3 & (mod5) \\\\ 2^{4} & ≡3 \\cdot 2≡6≡1 \\cdot 5 + 1≡1 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $2 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$, which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $2$ (modulo $5$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $3$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & ≡1 & (mod5) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 2x & ≡3 \\cdot 1 & (mod5) \\\\ (3 \\cdot 2)x & ≡3 & (mod5) \\\\ x & ≡3 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we get one solution, $x \\equiv 3 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:5)$.\n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $20$ (not modulo $5$). To find all the solutions modulo $20$, we repeatedly add $5$ modulo $20$ until we reach the number $3$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & ≡3 & (mod20) \\\\ x_{2} & ≡3 + 5≡8 & (mod20) \\\\ x_{3} & ≡8 + 5≡13 & (mod20) \\\\ x_{4} & ≡13 + 5≡18 & (mod20) \\\\ x_{5} & ≡18 + 5≡23≡1 \\cdot 20 + 3≡3≡x_{1} & (mod20)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x\\equiv 3, 8, 13, 18 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:20)$.\n\nFinally,\n$3+8+13+18 \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:20)$." "In the given congruence, we have $a=8$ and $n=20$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,20)=4\n$$\n\nSince $b=4$ is divisible by $4$, our congruence has $4$ solutions.\n\nFirst, we reduce the congruence using the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle 8 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  4 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 20 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 4 \\cdot  2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  4 \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 4 \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{4} \\cdot  2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\cancel{4} \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} \\cancel{4} \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  1 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNotice that in the new congruence, we have $a_1=2$ and $n_1=5$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=\\text{gcd}(2,5)=1\n$$\n\nThis means our congruence has a unique solution modulo $5$.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$. To find the inverse of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$, we compute the exponents of $2\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$ until we get a result of $1 \\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2^{2} & ≡4 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 2^{3} & ≡4 \\cdot 2≡8≡1 \\cdot 5 + 3≡3 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 2^{4} & ≡3 \\cdot 2≡6≡1 \\cdot 5 + 1≡1 & (\\bmod 5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, $2 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\:5)$, which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $2$ (modulo $5$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our congruence by $3$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x & ≡1 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 2x & ≡3 \\cdot 1 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ (3 \\cdot 2)x & ≡3 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ x & ≡3 & (\\bmod 5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we get one solution, $x \\equiv 3 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:5)$.\n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $20$ (not modulo $5$). To find all the solutions modulo $20$, we repeatedly add $5$ modulo $20$ until we reach the number $3$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & ≡3 & (\\bmod 20) \\\\ x_{2} & ≡3 + 5≡8 & (\\bmod 20) \\\\ x_{3} & ≡8 + 5≡13 & (\\bmod 20) \\\\ x_{4} & ≡13 + 5≡18 & (\\bmod 20) \\\\ x_{5} & ≡18 + 5≡23≡1 \\cdot 20 + 3≡3≡x_{1} & (\\bmod 20)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x\\equiv 3, 8, 13, 18 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:20)$.\n\nFinally,\n$3+8+13+18 \\equiv 2 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:20)$."] [:db/cas 17592186151869 :question/worked-solution "In the given congruence, we have $a=50$ and $n=298$, and we're given that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(50,298)=2\n$$\n\nSince $b=120$ is divisible by $2$, our congruence has $2$ solutions.\n\nNext, we use the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle 50 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  120 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 298 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 \\cdot  25 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  2 \\cdot  60 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 2 \\cdot  149 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{2} \\cdot  25 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\cancel{2} \\cdot  60 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} \\cancel{2} \\cdot  149 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 25 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  60 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 149 )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNotice that in this new congruence, we have $a_1=25$ and $n_1=149$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=\\text{gcd}(25,149)=1\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence has a unique solution modulo $149$.\n\nTo find the solution, we must multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $25\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:149)$. To find the inverse of $25\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:149)$, we apply the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{ccccccccc}\\displaystyle 149 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 25 \\cdot  5 & \\displaystyle + & \\displaystyle 24 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} 24 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 149 - 25 \\cdot  5 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  &  &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 25 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 24 \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle + & \\displaystyle 1 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} 1 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 25 - 24 \\cdot  1 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  &  &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 24 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 24 \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle + & \\displaystyle 0 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\phantom{\\Leftrightarrow} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\phantom{0} & \\displaystyle \\phantom{=} & \\displaystyle \\phantom{14 - 14 \\cdot  1}\\end{array}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, back-substituting in the usual way gives the following:\n\n$$\n1 = 6\\cdot 25 -149\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $149$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\cdot 25 - 149 & ≡1 & (mod149) \\\\ 6 \\cdot 25 & ≡1 & (mod149)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse of $25$ modulo $149$ is $6$.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $6$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}25x & ≡60 & (mod149) \\\\ 6 \\cdot 25x & ≡6 \\cdot 60 & (mod149) \\\\ x & ≡360 & (mod149) \\\\ x & ≡149 \\cdot 2 + 62 & (mod149) \\\\ x & ≡62 & (mod149)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the solution $x \\equiv 62 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:149)$.\n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $298$ (not modulo $149$). To find all the solutions modulo $298$, we repeatedly add $149$ to $62$ modulo $298$ until we reach the number $62$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & ≡62 & (mod298) \\\\ x_{2} & ≡62 + 149≡211 & (mod298) \\\\ x_{3} & ≡211 + 149≡360≡149 \\cdot 2 + 62≡x_{1} & (mod298)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx_{1}=62\\: (\\text{mod}\\:298) \\quad\\text{and}\\quad x_{2}= 211 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:298)\n$$" "In the given congruence, we have $a=50$ and $n=298$, and we're given that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(50,298)=2\n$$\n\nSince $b=120$ is divisible by $2$, our congruence has $2$ solutions.\n\nNext, we use the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle 50 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  120 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 298 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 \\cdot  25 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  2 \\cdot  60 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 2 \\cdot  149 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{2} \\cdot  25 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\cancel{2} \\cdot  60 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} \\cancel{2} \\cdot  149 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 25 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  60 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 149 )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNotice that in this new congruence, we have $a_1=25$ and $n_1=149$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a_1,n_1)=\\text{gcd}(25,149)=1\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence has a unique solution modulo $149$.\n\nTo find the solution, we must multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $25\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:149)$. To find the inverse of $25\\,(\\textrm{mod}\\:149)$, we apply the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{r}\\displaystyle \\begin{array}{ccccccccc}\\displaystyle 149 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 25 \\cdot  5 & \\displaystyle + & \\displaystyle 24 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} 24 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 149 - 25 \\cdot  5 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  &  &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 25 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 24 \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle + & \\displaystyle 1 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\Leftrightarrow  & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} 1 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 25 - 24 \\cdot  1 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle ↙ & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  &  &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 24 & \\displaystyle = & \\displaystyle 24 \\cdot  1 & \\displaystyle + & \\displaystyle 0 \\hspace{2em} & \\displaystyle \\phantom{\\Leftrightarrow} & \\displaystyle \\hspace{2em} \\phantom{0} & \\displaystyle \\phantom{=} & \\displaystyle \\phantom{14 - 14 \\cdot  1}\\end{array}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, back-substituting in the usual way gives the following:\n\n$$\n1 = 6\\cdot 25 -149\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $149$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\cdot 25 - 149 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 149) \\\\ 6 \\cdot 25 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 149)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse of $25$ modulo $149$ is $6$.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $6$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}25x & ≡60 & (\\bmod 149) \\\\ 6 \\cdot 25x & ≡6 \\cdot 60 & (\\bmod 149) \\\\ x & ≡360 & (\\bmod 149) \\\\ x & ≡149 \\cdot 2 + 62 & (\\bmod 149) \\\\ x & ≡62 & (\\bmod 149)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the solution $x \\equiv 62 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:149)$.\n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $298$ (not modulo $149$). To find all the solutions modulo $298$, we repeatedly add $149$ to $62$ modulo $298$ until we reach the number $62$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & ≡62 & (\\bmod 298) \\\\ x_{2} & ≡62 + 149≡211 & (\\bmod 298) \\\\ x_{3} & ≡211 + 149≡360≡149 \\cdot 2 + 62≡x_{1} & (\\bmod 298)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx_{1}=62\\: (\\text{mod}\\:298) \\quad\\text{and}\\quad x_{2}= 211 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:298)\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186151949 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta \\sin{\\theta}$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}4.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =y^2-x$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{ }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta \\sin{\\theta}$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}4.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) =y^2-x$, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\text{ }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta ({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -1) \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta (-{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-x)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186151950 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta$, where $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-(0-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos\\theta$ and $y=\\sin\\theta$, where $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2+y^2}{y^2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & ={\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{\\csc }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-(0-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186151951 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\cos\\theta,$ $\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{cos}^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}-{u}^{3}(1-{u}^{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{3}-{u}^{7})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x,y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}  f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta) \\,\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\cos^2\\theta$ and $y=\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta$, where $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y) = xy(1-x^2)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}) \\\\  & ={\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy(1-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta )\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\cos\\theta,$ $\\textrm d u=-\\sin\\theta\\,\\textrm d \\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\cos }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-{\\cos }^{4}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}-{u}^{3}(1-{u}^{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{3}-{u}^{7})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{u}^{8}}{8}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{8})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186151952 :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f: X \\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $f(x)=1-x^2$ has two roots, $x=\\pm 1.$ So, we have $f(-1)=0=f(1),$ while $-1 \\neq 1.$\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\sin x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $g(x) = 2.$\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, $h$ is a surjection because $\\forall\\, y \\in (0, \\infty),$ we can choose an $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $h(x)=y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ {3}^{x} & =y \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(y) \\\\ x & ={log}_{3}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify the above formula:\n\n$$\nh(x) = h \\left(\\log_3\\left(y\\right) \\right) = 3^{\\log_3\\left(y\\right)}  = y\n$$\n\nSecond, $h$ is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ {3}^{{x}_{1}} & ={3}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, we conclude that $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "Recall that a function $f: X \\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $f(x)=1-x^2$ has two roots, $x=\\pm 1.$ So, we have $f(-1)=0=f(1),$ while $-1 \\neq 1.$\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\sin x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\sin x \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $g(x) = 2.$\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, $h$ is a surjection because $\\forall\\, y \\in (0, \\infty),$ we can choose an $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $h(x)=y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ {3}^{x} & =y \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{x}) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(y) \\\\ x & ={\\log }_{3}⁡(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify the above formula:\n\n$$\nh(x) = h \\left(\\log_3\\left(y\\right) \\right) = 3^{\\log_3\\left(y\\right)}  = y\n$$\n\nSecond, $h$ is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ {3}^{{x}_{1}} & ={3}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={\\log }_{3}⁡({3}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, we conclude that $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186151979 :question/worked-solution "To encrypt a word using the Caesar cipher with key $k=16$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Add $k=16$ to each index modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0 + 16≡16 & (mod26) \\\\ 11 + 16≡27≡1 + 26≡1 & (mod26) \\\\ 1 + 16≡17 & (mod26) \\\\ 20 + 16≡36≡10 + 26≡10 & (mod26) \\\\ 12 + 16≡28≡2 + 26≡2 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{QBRKC}$." "To encrypt a word using the Caesar cipher with key $k=16$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Add $k=16$ to each index modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}0 + 16≡16 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 11 + 16≡27≡1 + 26≡1 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 1 + 16≡17 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 20 + 16≡36≡10 + 26≡10 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 12 + 16≡28≡2 + 26≡2 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{QBRKC}$."] [:db/cas 17592186151980 :question/worked-solution "To decipher a message encrypted using the Caesar cipher with key $k=14$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Subtract $k=14$ from each index modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}16 - 14≡2 & (mod26) \\\\ 2 - 14≡ - 12≡ - 12 + 26≡14 & (mod26) \\\\ 1 - 14≡ - 13≡ - 13 + 26≡13 & (mod26) \\\\ 18 - 14≡4 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{CONE}$." "To decipher a message encrypted using the Caesar cipher with key $k=14$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}18$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Subtract $k=14$ from each index modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}16 - 14≡2 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 2 - 14≡ - 12≡ - 12 + 26≡14 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 1 - 14≡ - 13≡ - 13 + 26≡13 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 18 - 14≡4 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{CONE}$."] [:db/cas 17592186151987 :question/worked-solution "To encrypt a word using the linear cipher with key $a=7$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Multiply each index by $a=7$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 19≡133≡3 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 0≡0 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 11≡77≡25 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 10≡70≡18 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{DAZS}$." "To encrypt a word using the linear cipher with key $a=7$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Multiply each index by $a=7$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 19≡133≡3 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 0≡0 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 11≡77≡25 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 10≡70≡18 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}18$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{DAZS}$."] [:db/cas 17592186151988 :question/worked-solution "To decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=15$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse to $a=15$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 15\\cdot 1 & + & 11 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 11 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 4 & = & 3\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =26-15 \\\\ 4 & =15-11 \\\\ 3 & =11-4\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =4-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4-3 \\\\  & =4-(11-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =(15-11)\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-11\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-(26-15)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 7-26\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 7+26\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 7 + 26 \\cdot (-4) & ≡1 & (mod26) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 7 & ≡1 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $15 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=15$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=7$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=7$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 25≡175≡19 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 0≡0≡0 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 9≡63≡11 & (mod26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 20≡140≡10 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{TALK}$." "To decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=15$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse to $a=15$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 15\\cdot 1 & + & 11 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 15 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 11 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 4 & = & 3\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =26-15 \\\\ 4 & =15-11 \\\\ 3 & =11-4\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =4-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =4-3 \\\\  & =4-(11-4\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =(15-11)\\cdot 3-11 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-11\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 3-(26-15)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 7-26\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 7+26\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15 \\cdot 7 + 26 \\cdot (-4) & ≡1 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 15 \\cdot 7 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $15 \\cdot 7 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=15$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=7$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=7$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 25≡175≡19 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 0≡0≡0 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 9≡63≡11 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 20≡140≡10 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}10$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\text{TALK}$."] [:db/cas 17592186151990 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152015 :question/worked-solution "The special case of Fermat's little theorem states the following:\n\n> $a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:p)$ for any prime $p$ and any integer $a$ that is coprime with $p$.\n\nFirst, notice that $13$ is prime and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(4,13)=1\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the special case of Fermat's little theorem:\n\n$$\n4^{13-1} \\equiv 4^{\\color{blue}12} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:13)\n$$\n\nLet's now find $7^{100}$ modulo ${\\color{blue}12}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 7^{1}\\begin{vmatrix}≡7 & (mod12) \\\\ 7^{2} & ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(mod12); 7^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}≡7 & (mod12) \\\\ 7^{4} & ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(mod12); ⋮\n$$\nThis pattern will continue. So, $7$ to the power of any even integer (like $100$) will give us $1$ modulo $12$, and we can write\n\n$$\n7^{100} = 12k+1\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nNow, re-writing the given exponent, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{7}^{100}} & ≡{4}^{12k+1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡{({4}^{12})}^{k}\\cdot {4}^{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡{(1)}^{k}\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The special case of Fermat's little theorem states the following:\n\n> $a^{p-1} \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:p)$ for any prime $p$ and any integer $a$ that is coprime with $p$.\n\nFirst, notice that $13$ is prime and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(4,13)=1\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the special case of Fermat's little theorem:\n\n$$\n4^{13-1} \\equiv 4^{\\color{blue}12} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:13)\n$$\n\nLet's now find $7^{100}$ modulo ${\\color{blue}12}$. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 7^{1}\\begin{vmatrix}≡7 & (\\bmod 12) \\\\ 7^{2} & ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(\\bmod 12); 7^{3}\\begin{vmatrix}≡7 & (\\bmod 12) \\\\ 7^{4} & ≡1\\end{vmatrix}(\\bmod 12); ⋮\n$$\nThis pattern will continue. So, $7$ to the power of any even integer (like $100$) will give us $1$ modulo $12$, and we can write\n\n$$\n7^{100} = 12k+1\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$.\n\nNow, re-writing the given exponent, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{{7}^{100}} & ≡{4}^{12k+1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡{({4}^{12})}^{k}\\cdot {4}^{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡{(1)}^{k}\\cdot 4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13) \\\\  & ≡4 &  & (\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}13)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152017 :question/worked-solution "Euler's theorem states that if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\na^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\phi$ is Euler's totient function.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{gcd}(5,39)=1$.\n- Statement II is true. First, notice that\n$39 = 3 \\cdot 13$,\nwhere $3$ and $13$ are distinct prime numbers. As a result,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ(39) & =ϕ(3)\\cdot ϕ(13) \\\\  & =(3-1)\\cdot (13-1) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Statement III is true. By Euler's theorem, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a^{ϕ(n)} & ≡1 & (modn) \\\\ 5^{ϕ(39)} & ≡1 & (mod39) \\\\ 5^{24} & ≡1 & (mod39)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "Euler's theorem states that if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, then\n\n$$\na^{\\phi(n)} \\equiv 1 \\quad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\phi$ is Euler's totient function.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\text{gcd}(5,39)=1$.\n- Statement II is true. First, notice that\n$39 = 3 \\cdot 13$,\nwhere $3$ and $13$ are distinct prime numbers. As a result,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ(39) & =ϕ(3)\\cdot ϕ(13) \\\\  & =(3-1)\\cdot (13-1) \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Statement III is true. By Euler's theorem, we get the following:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}a^{ϕ(n)} & ≡1 & (modn) \\\\ 5^{ϕ(39)} & ≡1 & (\\bmod 39) \\\\ 5^{24} & ≡1 & (\\bmod 39)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""] [:db/cas 17592186152406 :question/problem "Which of the following congruences have *precisely three* solutions modulo $24$?\n\n1. $18x≡12(mod24)$\n2. $9x≡5(mod24)$\n3. $15x≡18(mod24)$" "Which of the following congruences have *precisely three* solutions modulo $24$?\n\n1. $18x≡12(\\bmod 24)$\n2. $9x≡5(\\bmod 24)$\n3. $15x≡18(\\bmod 24)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152407 :question/problem "Find the sum modulo $18$ of the solutions to the congruence $8x≡14(mod18)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" "Find the sum modulo $18$ of the solutions to the congruence $8x≡14(\\bmod 18)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*"] [:db/cas 17592186152472 :question/problem "Given that $a≡b(mod14)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$3$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" "Given that $a≡b(\\bmod 14)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$3$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186152474 :question/problem "Given that $a≡b(mod7)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$15$ {{field-1}}" "Given that $a≡b(\\bmod 7)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$15$ {{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186152475 :question/problem "Given that $a≡b(mod14)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$3$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" "Given that $a≡b(\\bmod 14)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$3$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186152479 :question/problem "Given that $6a≡6b(mod14)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$3$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" "Given that $6a≡6b(\\bmod 14)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$3$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}"] [:db/cas 17592186152487 :question/problem "Given that $a≡b(mod9)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$5$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" "Given that $a≡b(\\bmod 9)$, construct a statement that also must be true:$5$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}"] [:db/cas 17592186152490 :question/problem "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe integer $21$\n{{field-1}}\nthe additive inverse of $15$ modulo $34$ since $21 + 15$ {{field-2}} $(mod34)$." "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe integer $21$\n{{field-1}}\nthe additive inverse of $15$ modulo $34$ since $21 + 15$ {{field-2}} $(\\bmod 34)$."] [:db/cas 17592186152496 :question/problem "Find the solutions of $132x≡12(mod441)$. Express your answers as nonnegative integers smaller than $441$, and in ascending order.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $gcd(132, 441) = 3$.*\n\n$x_{1}≡$\n{{field-1}}\n$, x_{2}≡$\n{{field-2}}\n$, x_{3}≡$\n{{field-3}}\n$(mod441)$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "Find the solutions of $132x≡12(\\bmod 441)$. Express your answers as nonnegative integers smaller than $441$, and in ascending order.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\gcd (132, 441) = 3$.*\n\n$x_{1}≡$\n{{field-1}}\n$, x_{2}≡$\n{{field-2}}\n$, x_{3}≡$\n{{field-3}}\n$(\\bmod 441)$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186152501 :question/problem "Which of the following are equivalence relations on $Z$?\n\n1. $x < y$\n2. $x∤y$\n3. $x≡y(mod6)$" "Which of the following are equivalence relations on $Z$?\n\n1. $x < y$\n2. $x∤y$\n3. $x≡y(\\bmod 6)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152504 :question/problem "Find the solutions of $92x≡24(mod284)$. Express your answers as nonnegative integers smaller than $284$, and in ascending order.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $gcd(92, 284) = 4$.*\n\n$x_{1}≡$\n{{field-1}}\n$, x_{2}≡$\n{{field-2}}\n$, x_{3}≡$\n{{field-3}}\n$, x_{4}≡$\n{{field-4}}\n$(mod284)$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." "Find the solutions of $92x≡24(\\bmod 284)$. Express your answers as nonnegative integers smaller than $284$, and in ascending order.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\gcd (92, 284) = 4$.*\n\n$x_{1}≡$\n{{field-1}}\n$, x_{2}≡$\n{{field-2}}\n$, x_{3}≡$\n{{field-3}}\n$, x_{4}≡$\n{{field-4}}\n$(\\bmod 284)$\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186152598 :question/problem "If $Re(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ and $Im(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}$ are the real and imaginary parts of the vector $x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 - 5\\textrm{i} \\\\ 6 - 3\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $ab + cd$?" "If $\\Re (x) = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\Im (x) = \\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}$ are the real and imaginary parts of the vector $x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 - 5\\textrm{i} \\\\ 6 - 3\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of $ab + cd$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152599 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{4{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4({cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }, \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$ and $y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }$, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x+y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\frac{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4{\\cos }^{2}⁡2\\theta +4{\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4({\\cos }^{2}⁡2\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =4([\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]-[-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}]) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152690 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152732 :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- “$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$” is a surjection. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- “$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only real solutions of $g(x)={x}^{2}=2$ are $x=\\pm\\sqrt{2}$, and neither belongs to $Q$.\n\n- “$h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={x}^{2}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={x}^{2}=-1.$  In fact, ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in R$, with equality at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$” only." "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- “$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$” is a surjection. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ choose $x={\\log }_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $f(x)=f({\\log }_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{\\log }_{3}⁡y}=y.$\n\n- “$g:Q\\to Q$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Q.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only real solutions of $g(x)={x}^{2}=2$ are $x=\\pm\\sqrt{2}$, and neither belongs to $Q$.\n\n- “$h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)={x}^{2}$” is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={x}^{2}=-1.$  In fact, ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in R$, with equality at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $f(x)={3}^{x}$” only."] [:db/cas 17592186152739 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=5{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 5{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =25{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}25{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =125{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}125{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =125{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =125\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{4}({1}^{4}-{0}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=5{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 5{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =25{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}25{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =125{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}125{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\sin }^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =125{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =125\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{u}^{4}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{4}({1}^{4}-{0}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152740 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & ={(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +4{\\sin }^{4}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +4{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }4{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152743 :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\sqrt{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152748 :question/problem "What is $37mod5$?" "What is $37\\bmod 5$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152750 :question/problem "What is $-8mod5$?" "What is $-8\\bmod 5$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152756 :question/problem "What is $45mod7$?" "What is $45\\bmod 7$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152757 :question/problem "What is $-15mod7$?" "What is $-15\\bmod 7$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152765 :question/problem "Given that $2a≡2b(mod14)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a≡b(mod7)$\n2. $a≡b(mod14)$\n3. $5a≡5b(mod7)$" "Given that $2a≡2b(\\bmod 14)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a≡b(\\bmod 7)$\n2. $a≡b(\\bmod 14)$\n3. $5a≡5b(\\bmod 7)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152766 :question/problem "Given that $3a≡3b(mod6)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a≡b(mod2)$\n2. $a≡b(mod6)$\n3. $a≡b(mod3)$" "Given that $3a≡3b(\\bmod 6)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a≡b(\\bmod 2)$\n2. $a≡b(\\bmod 6)$\n3. $a≡b(\\bmod 3)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152768 :question/problem "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $53u + 12v = gcd(53, 12)$. What is the value of $u + v$?" "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $53u + 12v = \\gcd (53, 12)$. What is the value of $u + v$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152769 :question/problem "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $56u + 21v = gcd(56, 21)$. What is the value of $u + v$?" "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $56u + 21v = \\gcd (56, 21)$. What is the value of $u + v$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152781 :question/problem "Use trial and error to find the solutions, if any, to the congruence $4x≡2(mod8)$." "Use trial and error to find the solutions, if any, to the congruence $4x≡2(\\bmod 8)$."] [:db/cas 17592186152782 :question/problem "Use trial and error to find the solutions, if any, to the congruence $3x≡3(mod9)$." "Use trial and error to find the solutions, if any, to the congruence $3x≡3(\\bmod 9)$."] [:db/cas 17592186152783 :question/problem "Which of the following linear congruences do **NOT** have any solutions?\n\n1. $3x≡12(mod24)$\n2. $3x≡7(mod24)$\n3. $3x≡2(mod20)$" "Which of the following linear congruences do **NOT** have any solutions?\n\n1. $3x≡12(\\bmod 24)$\n2. $3x≡7(\\bmod 24)$\n3. $3x≡2(\\bmod 20)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152784 :question/problem "Find the sum modulo $30$ of the solutions to the congruence $9x≡6(mod30)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*" "Find the sum modulo $30$ of the solutions to the congruence $9x≡6(\\bmod 30)$.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use trial and error to find the appropriate multiplicative inverse.*"] [:db/cas 17592186152804 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1.\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\ \\frac{x+1}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 \\\\ y-1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2} & ={\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{16}+(y-1)^{2}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152811 :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={sin}^{2}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ only." "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={\\sin }^{2}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-1.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186152839 :question/problem "Find $Im(k)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle v = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle kv^(―) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\textrm{i} + 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" "Find $\\Im (k)$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle v = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$$\n\\displaystyle kv^(―) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\textrm{i} + 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186152843 :question/problem "What is $3^{113}mod11$?" "What is $3^{113}\\bmod 11$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152844 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true according to Fermat's little theorem?\n\n1. $6^{6}≡1(mod7)$\n2. $8^{17}≡8(mod17)$\n3. $7^{31}≡1(mod32)$" "Which of the following statements are true according to Fermat's little theorem?\n\n1. $6^{6}≡1(\\bmod 7)$\n2. $8^{17}≡8(\\bmod 17)$\n3. $7^{31}≡1(\\bmod 32)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152845 :question/problem "What is $5^{2^{100}}mod7$?" "What is $5^{2^{100}}\\bmod 7$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152846 :question/problem "What is $4^{122}mod7$?" "What is $4^{122}\\bmod 7$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152847 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true according to Fermat's little theorem?\n\n1. $3^{19}≡2(mod19)$\n2. $4^{10}≡1(mod11)$\n3. $7^{15}≡1(mod16)$" "Which of the following statements are true according to Fermat's little theorem?\n\n1. $3^{19}≡2(\\bmod 19)$\n2. $4^{10}≡1(\\bmod 11)$\n3. $7^{15}≡1(\\bmod 16)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152848 :question/problem "What is $3^{3^{100}}mod7$?" "What is $3^{3^{100}}\\bmod 7$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152855 :question/problem "Consider the following congruence, which is either true or false:\n\n$2^{24}≡1(mod35)$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $gcd(2, 35) = 1$\n2. $ϕ(35) = 24$\n3. The congruence is true by Euler's theorem" "Consider the following congruence, which is either true or false:\n\n$2^{24}≡1(\\bmod 35)$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\gcd (2, 35) = 1$\n2. $ϕ(35) = 24$\n3. The congruence is true by Euler's theorem"] [:db/cas 17592186152856 :question/problem "Consider the following congruence, which is either true or false:\n\n$2^{12}≡1(mod26)$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $gcd(2, 26) = 1$\n2. $ϕ(26) = 12$\n3. The congruence is true by Euler's theorem" "Consider the following congruence, which is either true or false:\n\n$2^{12}≡1(\\bmod 26)$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\gcd (2, 26) = 1$\n2. $ϕ(26) = 12$\n3. The congruence is true by Euler's theorem"] [:db/cas 17592186152857 :question/problem "What is $3^{117}mod14$?" "What is $3^{117}\\bmod 14$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152858 :question/problem "What is $4^{6^{500}}mod15$?" "What is $4^{6^{500}}\\bmod 15$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152859 :question/problem "What is $4^{108}mod15$?" "What is $4^{108}\\bmod 15$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152860 :question/problem "What is $3^{5^{550}}mod14$?" "What is $3^{5^{550}}\\bmod 14$?"] [:db/cas 17592186152866 :question/problem "Given that $6a≡6b(mod12)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a≡b(mod4)$\n2. $a≡b(mod2)$\n3. $3a≡3b(mod6)$" "Given that $6a≡6b(\\bmod 12)$, which of the following statements **must** be true?\n\n1. $a≡b(\\bmod 4)$\n2. $a≡b(\\bmod 2)$\n3. $3a≡3b(\\bmod 6)$"] [:db/cas 17592186152879 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $gcd(888, 205) = 7$\n2. $gcd(888, 205) = 1$\n3. $888$ and $205$ are coprime" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\gcd (888, 205) = 7$\n2. $\\gcd (888, 205) = 1$\n3. $888$ and $205$ are coprime"] [:db/cas 17592186152880 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $gcd(6021, 4023) = 1$\n2. $gcd(6021, 4023) = 27$\n3. $6021$ and $4023$ are not coprime" "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\gcd (6021, 4023) = 1$\n2. $\\gcd (6021, 4023) = 27$\n3. $6021$ and $4023$ are not coprime"] [:db/cas 17592186153385 :tutorial/content "The **linear cipher** is similar to the Caesar cipher. However, instead of adding the key, we multiply by the key. If the alphabet consists of $n$ letters, and the key is $a$, then the plain text index $i_\\textrm{plain}$ is translated into the ciphertext index $i_\\textrm{cipher}$ as follows:\n\n$$\na \\cdot i_\\textrm{plain} \\equiv i_\\textrm{cipher} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFor example, let's encrypt the message $\\textrm{HEY}$ using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters. First, we recall the indices of letters in the English alphabet:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Multiply each index by $a=3$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 7≡21 & (mod26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 4≡12 & (mod26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 24≡72≡20 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the encrypted message is $\\text{VMU}$." "The **linear cipher** is similar to the Caesar cipher. However, instead of adding the key, we multiply by the key. If the alphabet consists of $n$ letters, and the key is $a$, then the plain text index $i_\\textrm{plain}$ is translated into the ciphertext index $i_\\textrm{cipher}$ as follows:\n\n$$\na \\cdot i_\\textrm{plain} \\equiv i_\\textrm{cipher} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFor example, let's encrypt the message $\\textrm{HEY}$ using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters. First, we recall the indices of letters in the English alphabet:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNext, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Multiply each index by $a=3$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 7≡21 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 4≡12 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 24≡72≡20 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the encrypted message is $\\text{VMU}$."] [:db/cas 17592186153386 :tutorial/content "A **cipher** is a procedure for transforming or **encrypting** a message to conceal its meaning. The original message is said to be written in **plain text**, while the encrypted message is said to be written in **ciphertext**.\n\nCiphers often work by\n\n1. translating each letter into a number called an **index**,\n2. performing an operation on the index, and then\n3. translating the number back into a letter.\n\nThe letters in the English alphabet are indexed as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOne of the simplest ciphers, the **Caesar cipher**, involves replacing each letter in a message with a different one $k$ places down the alphabet. If the alphabet consists of $n$ letters, then the plain text index $i_\\textrm{plain}$ is translated into the ciphertext index $i_\\textrm{cipher}$ as follows:\n\n$$\ni_\\textrm{plain} + k \\equiv i_\\textrm{cipher} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFor example, to encrypt the message $\\textrm{HEY}$ using the Caesar cipher with the **key** $k=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Add $k=3$ to each index modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 + 3≡10 & (mod26) \\\\ 4 + 3≡7 & (mod26) \\\\ 24 + 3≡27≡1 + 26≡1 & (mod26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the encrypted message is $\\text{KHB}$." "A **cipher** is a procedure for transforming or **encrypting** a message to conceal its meaning. The original message is said to be written in **plain text**, while the encrypted message is said to be written in **ciphertext**.\n\nCiphers often work by\n\n1. translating each letter into a number called an **index**,\n2. performing an operation on the index, and then\n3. translating the number back into a letter.\n\nThe letters in the English alphabet are indexed as follows:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$11$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">N</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">O</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">P</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Q</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">S</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">W</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">X</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nOne of the simplest ciphers, the **Caesar cipher**, involves replacing each letter in a message with a different one $k$ places down the alphabet. If the alphabet consists of $n$ letters, then the plain text index $i_\\textrm{plain}$ is translated into the ciphertext index $i_\\textrm{cipher}$ as follows:\n\n$$\ni_\\textrm{plain} + k \\equiv i_\\textrm{cipher} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nFor example, to encrypt the message $\\textrm{HEY}$ using the Caesar cipher with the **key** $k=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Add $k=3$ to each index modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 + 3≡10 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 4 + 3≡7 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 24 + 3≡27≡1 + 26≡1 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}10$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the encrypted message is $\\text{KHB}$."] [:db/cas 17592186153387 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we encrypted a message using the Caesar cipher with key $k=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters. The resulting encrypted message was $\\textrm{KHB}$.\n\nHowever, suppose we send this message to a friend. How can they **decipher** or **decrypt** this message to reveal the original message?\n\nDecrypting is the opposite of encrypting. Remember that to encrypt a message, we add the key to the indices of the letters. So, to *decrypt* a message, all we have to do is *subtract* the key:\n\n$$\ni_\\textrm{cipher} - k \\equiv i_\\textrm{plain} \\: (\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nLet's recall the indices of the English alphabet and then decipher the message.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\