ta-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Z</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$13$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$14$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$19$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$20$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$23$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$24$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo decipher a message encrypted using the linear cipher with key $a=3$ over the English alphabet with $n=26$ letters, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the ciphertext in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">V</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">U</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}21$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{red}20$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Find the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}26 & = & 3\\cdot 8 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 2\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =26-3\\cdot 8 \\\\ 1 & =3-2\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we back-substitute:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =3-2 \\\\  & =3-(26-3\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9-26 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 9+26\\cdot (-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe can write this result in modulo $26$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\cdot 9 + 26 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 3 \\cdot 9 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $3 \\cdot 9 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:26)$, which means that the inverse of $a=3$ modulo $26$ is $a^{-1}=9$.\n- Multiply each index by $a^{-1}=9$ modulo $n=26$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\cdot 21≡189≡7 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 12≡108≡4 & (\\bmod 26) \\\\ 9 \\cdot 20≡180≡24 & (\\bmod 26)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indices:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}7$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}24$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">Y</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nWe successfully recovered the original message, which was $\\text{HEY}$."] [:db/cas 17592186153390 :tutorial/content "How do we determine whether a function $f$ is an injection without using a graph or mapping diagram? \n\nTo do this, we take $x_1$ and $x_2$ (arbitrary but fixed) in the domain of $f,$ and we construct the equation $f(x_1)=f(x_2).$ Using algebraic manipulation, we aim to get some relation between the variables $x_1$ and $x_2.$ If we get $x_1=x_2,$ this implies that $f$ is an injection. \n\nLet's use this idea to prove that $f:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow (0,\\infty)$ defined by $f(x)=2^x$ is an injection.\n\nFirst, we construct the equation $f(x_1) = f(x_2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using algebraic manipulation, we reduce this relationship to $x_1=x_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for any $x_1, x_2 \\in\\mathbb{R},$ if $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$ we have $x_1=x_2.$ In other words, $f$ is an injection." "How do we determine whether a function $f$ is an injection without using a graph or mapping diagram? \n\nTo do this, we take $x_1$ and $x_2$ (arbitrary but fixed) in the domain of $f,$ and we construct the equation $f(x_1)=f(x_2).$ Using algebraic manipulation, we aim to get some relation between the variables $x_1$ and $x_2.$ If we get $x_1=x_2,$ this implies that $f$ is an injection. \n\nLet's use this idea to prove that $f:\\mathbb{R}\\rightarrow (0,\\infty)$ defined by $f(x)=2^x$ is an injection.\n\nFirst, we construct the equation $f(x_1) = f(x_2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using algebraic manipulation, we reduce this relationship to $x_1=x_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{x}_{1}} & ={2}^{{x}_{2}} \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{1}}) & ={\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{{x}_{2}}) \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for any $x_1, x_2 \\in\\mathbb{R},$ if $f(x_1)=f(x_2)$ we have $x_1=x_2.$ In other words, $f$ is an injection."] [:db/cas 17592186153462 :tutorial/content "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\nax \\equiv b \\qquad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nIn cases where $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n) > 1$ and $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n) \\mid b$, it's possible to reduce the congruence to a simpler congruence with a unique solution. However, the solution to the simpler congruence is only *part* of the solution to the original congruence. An extra step is needed to find the complete solution.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find all the solutions to the following congruence:\n\n$$\n2x \\equiv 6 \\qquad (\\text{mod}\\:10)\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=2$ and $n=10$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(2,10)=2\n$$\n\nSince $b=6$ is divisible by $2$, our congruence has $2$ solutions modulo $10$.\n\nNotice that we can simplify the congruence using the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle 2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  6 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 10 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 \\cdot  x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  2 \\cdot  3 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 2 \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{2} \\cdot  x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\cancel{2} \\cdot  3 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} \\cancel{2} \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  {\\color{blue}3} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThus, our simplified congruence has the unique solution, $x \\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{red}5})$.\n\nBut remember, we expect $2$ solutions modulo $10$. To find all the solutions, we repeatedly add the reduced modulus (${\\color{red}5}$) to our reduced solution (${\\color{blue}3}$) modulo $10$ until we reach our reduced solution (${\\color{blue}3}$) again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & ≡3 & (mod10) \\\\ x_{2} & ≡3 + 5≡8 & (mod10) \\\\ x_{3} & ≡8 + 5≡13≡10 + 3≡3 & (mod10)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x\\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\: (\\text{mod}\\:10)$ and $x\\equiv {\\color{purple}{8}} \\: (\\text{mod}\\:10)$." "Consider the following congruence:\n\n$$\nax \\equiv b \\qquad (\\text{mod}\\:n)\n$$\n\nIn cases where $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n) > 1$ and $\\textrm{gcd}(a,n) \\mid b$, it's possible to reduce the congruence to a simpler congruence with a unique solution. However, the solution to the simpler congruence is only *part* of the solution to the original congruence. An extra step is needed to find the complete solution.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's find all the solutions to the following congruence:\n\n$$\n2x \\equiv 6 \\qquad (\\text{mod}\\:10)\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=2$ and $n=10$, so\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(2,10)=2\n$$\n\nSince $b=6$ is divisible by $2$, our congruence has $2$ solutions modulo $10$.\n\nNotice that we can simplify the congruence using the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rlrl}\\displaystyle 2 x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  6 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 10 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle 2 \\cdot  x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  2 \\cdot  3 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 2 \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle \\cancel{2} \\cdot  x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  \\cancel{2} \\cdot  3 & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} \\cancel{2} \\cdot  5 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle x & \\displaystyle  \\equiv  {\\color{blue}3} & \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 )\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThus, our simplified congruence has the unique solution, $x \\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\: (\\text{mod}\\:{\\color{red}5})$.\n\nBut remember, we expect $2$ solutions modulo $10$. To find all the solutions, we repeatedly add the reduced modulus (${\\color{red}5}$) to our reduced solution (${\\color{blue}3}$) modulo $10$ until we reach our reduced solution (${\\color{blue}3}$) again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} & ≡3 & (\\bmod 10) \\\\ x_{2} & ≡3 + 5≡8 & (\\bmod 10) \\\\ x_{3} & ≡8 + 5≡13≡10 + 3≡3 & (\\bmod 10)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x\\equiv {\\color{blue}3} \\: (\\text{mod}\\:10)$ and $x\\equiv {\\color{purple}{8}} \\: (\\text{mod}\\:10)$."] [:db/cas 17592186153629 :answer/value "(37 \\cdot 71)mod73" "(37 \\cdot 71)\\bmod 73"] [:db/cas 17592186153630 :answer/value "(55 \\cdot 71)mod73" "(55 \\cdot 71)\\bmod 73"] [:db/cas 17592186153631 :answer/value "(55 \\cdot 37)mod73" "(55 \\cdot 37)\\bmod 73"] [:db/cas 17592186153632 :answer/value "(37 \\cdot 16)mod73" "(37 \\cdot 16)\\bmod 73"] [:db/cas 17592186153633 :answer/value "(55 \\cdot 16)mod73" "(55 \\cdot 16)\\bmod 73"] [:db/cas 17592186154591 :answer/value "gcd(5, 7)" "\\gcd (5, 7)"] [:db/cas 17592186154592 :answer/value "lcm(5, 7)" "\\operatorname{lcm} (5, 7)"] [:db/cas 17592186154894 :answer/value "(12 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9)mod37" "(12 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9)\\bmod 37"] [:db/cas 17592186154895 :answer/value "(7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 15)mod37" "(7 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 15)\\bmod 37"] [:db/cas 17592186154896 :answer/value "(33 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9)mod37" "(33 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 9)\\bmod 37"] [:db/cas 17592186154897 :answer/value "(12 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 3)mod37" "(12 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 3)\\bmod 37"] [:db/cas 17592186154898 :answer/value "(33 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 3)mod37" "(33 \\cdot 12 \\cdot 3)\\bmod 37"] [:db/cas 17592186156940 :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}min^{2}" "\\frac{11}{4}\\min ^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186156941 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}min^{2}" "\\frac{1}{8}\\min ^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186156942 :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}min^{2}" "\\frac{16}{3}\\min ^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186156943 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}min^{2}" "\\frac{1}{2}\\min ^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186156944 :answer/value "\\frac{26}{5}min^{2}" "\\frac{26}{5}\\min ^{2}"] [:db/cas 17592186157029 :question/worked-solution "To encrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the public key\n\n$$\n(e,n)=(19,65)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $e=19$ modulo $n=65$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1^{19}≡1 & (mod65) \\\\ 8^{19}≡57 & (mod65) \\\\ 19^{19}≡59 & (mod65)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ciphertext is\n$01 \\:\\: 57 \\:\\: 59$." "To encrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the public key\n\n$$\n(e,n)=(19,65)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Find the index of each letter of the word in the alphabet:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $e=19$ modulo $n=65$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1^{19}≡1 & (\\bmod 65) \\\\ 8^{19}≡57 & (\\bmod 65) \\\\ 19^{19}≡59 & (\\bmod 65)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ciphertext is\n$01 \\:\\: 57 \\:\\: 59$."] [:db/cas 17592186157030 :question/worked-solution "To decrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the private key\n\n$$\n(d,n)=(13,55)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $d=13$ modulo $n=55$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}52^{13}≡17 & (mod55) \\\\ 49^{13}≡4 & (mod55) \\\\ 53^{13}≡3 & (mod55)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the plaintext was\n$R \\:\\: E \\:\\: D$." "To decrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the private key\n\n$$\n(d,n)=(13,55)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $d=13$ modulo $n=55$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}52^{13}≡17 & (\\bmod 55) \\\\ 49^{13}≡4 & (\\bmod 55) \\\\ 53^{13}≡3 & (\\bmod 55)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}17$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">R</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the plaintext was\n$R \\:\\: E \\:\\: D$."] [:db/cas 17592186157031 :question/worked-solution "To decrypt a message using the RSA cipher, we need to know its corresponding private key.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nn=33 = 3 \\times 11\n$$\n\nLet's denote $p=3$ and $q=11$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(n) &= (p - 1)(q - 1) \\\\\n&= (3 - 1)(11 - 1) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the RSA cipher with the private key $(d,n)$, the first component $d$ is the multiplicative inverse of $e=7$ (the first component of the public key) modulo\n\n$$\n\\phi(n)=20{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nd = e^{-1} \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 20)\n$$\n\nSo, let's find the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $\\phi(n)=20$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}20 & = & 7\\cdot 2 & + & 6 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 7 & = & 6\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 &= 20 - 7 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1 &= 7 - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 7 - 6 \\\\\n&= 7 - (20 - 7 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 - 20 \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $20$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (mod20) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 3 & ≡1 & (mod20)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $7 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:20)$, which means that the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $20$ is $e^{-1}=3$.\n\nThus, our private key is\n\n$$\n(d,n) = (3,33)\n$$\n\nNow, to decrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the private key\n\n$$\n(d,n)=(3,33)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $d=3$ modulo $n=33$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15^{3}≡09 & (mod33) \\\\ 16^{3}≡04 & (mod33) \\\\ 13^{3}≡19 & (mod33)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}09$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}04$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the plaintext was\n$J \\:\\: E \\:\\: T$." "To decrypt a message using the RSA cipher, we need to know its corresponding private key.\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nn=33 = 3 \\times 11\n$$\n\nLet's denote $p=3$ and $q=11$. Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nϕ(n) &= (p - 1)(q - 1) \\\\\n&= (3 - 1)(11 - 1) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 10 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the RSA cipher with the private key $(d,n)$, the first component $d$ is the multiplicative inverse of $e=7$ (the first component of the public key) modulo\n\n$$\n\\phi(n)=20{:}\n$$\n\n$$\nd = e^{-1} \\quad (\\textrm{mod} \\: 20)\n$$\n\nSo, let's find the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $\\phi(n)=20$. To do that, we will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{array}{ccccc}20 & = & 7\\cdot 2 & + & 6 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 7 & = & 6\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{array}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 &= 20 - 7 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n1 &= 7 - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 7 - 6 \\\\\n&= 7 - (20 - 7 \\cdot 2) \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 - 20 \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $20$, as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\cdot 3 + 20 \\cdot (-1) & ≡1 & (\\bmod 20) \\\\ 7 \\cdot 3 & ≡1 & (\\bmod 20)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nSo we have $7 \\cdot 3 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\:20)$, which means that the inverse of $e=7$ modulo $20$ is $e^{-1}=3$.\n\nThus, our private key is\n\n$$\n(d,n) = (3,33)\n$$\n\nNow, to decrypt a word using the RSA cipher with the private key\n\n$$\n(d,n)=(3,33)\n$$\n\nover the English alphabet, we do the following:\n\n- Raise each index to the power of $d=3$ modulo $n=33$:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}15^{3}≡09 & (\\bmod 33) \\\\ 16^{3}≡04 & (\\bmod 33) \\\\ 13^{3}≡19 & (\\bmod 33)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n- Find the letters that correspond to the new indexes:\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}09$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}04$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\color{blue}19$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">T</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n\nTherefore, the plaintext was\n$J \\:\\: E \\:\\: T$."] [:db/cas 17592186157042 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_\\infty(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b\\|_\\infty\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v,$\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|_\\infty = \\operatorname{max}(|v_1|,|v_2|,\\ldots, |v_N|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|-2-8|,|6-(-9)|,|-7-(-9)|) \\\\  & =max(10,15,2) \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\nd_\\infty(\\mathbf a,\\mathbf b) =\\|\\mathbf a-\\mathbf b\\|_\\infty\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $\\mathbf v,$\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf v\\|_\\infty = \\operatorname{max}(|v_1|,|v_2|,\\ldots, |v_N|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\max (|-2-8|,|6-(-9)|,|-7-(-9)|) \\\\  & =\\max (10,15,2) \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157065 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the conditional statement $P \\Rightarrow Q$ and its contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nare logically equivalent. Moreover, it's sometimes easier to prove the contrapositive.\n\nIn this case, we need to show that\n$\\underbrace{a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{4a \\not\\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12)}_{Q}$.\nThe contrapositive of the statement is\n$\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(4a \\not\\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P}$,\nwhich is equivalent to\n$4a \\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> The contrapositive of the given statement is\n> $4a \\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\n> We will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the definition of modular congruence. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$. So, we can reason as follows:\n\n> By the definition of modular congruence,\n> $4a≡4b(mod12) \\mid ⇔12∣(4a - 4b); ⇔12∣4(a - b)$\n\nNext, we use the definition of divisibility:\n\n> By the definition of divisibility in $\\mathbb{Z}$, $12 \\mid 4(a-b)$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that\n> $4(a-b) = 12k$.\n\nNow, we can cancel out the factor of $4{:}$\n\n> Dividing this equation by $4$, we obtain\n> $a-b = 3k$,\n> which in turn implies\n> $3 \\mid (a-b)$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence again:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have\n> $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\n> By proving the contrapositive, we proved the initial statement, as required." "Recall that the conditional statement $P \\Rightarrow Q$ and its contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\nare logically equivalent. Moreover, it's sometimes easier to prove the contrapositive.\n\nIn this case, we need to show that\n$\\underbrace{a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)}_{P} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{4a \\not\\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12)}_{Q}$.\nThe contrapositive of the statement is\n$\\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(4a \\not\\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, Q} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{\\lnot \\Big(a \\not\\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3) \\Big)}_{\\lnot \\, P}$,\nwhich is equivalent to\n$4a \\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\nSo, we proceed as follows:\n\n> The contrapositive of the given statement is\n> $4a \\equiv 4b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 12) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\n> We will prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the definition of modular congruence. Recall that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: n) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad n \\mid (a-b) \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad a-b = nk\n$$\n\nfor some integer $k$. So, we can reason as follows:\n\n> By the definition of modular congruence,\n> $4a≡4b(\\bmod 12) \\mid ⇔12∣(4a - 4b); ⇔12∣4(a - b)$\n\nNext, we use the definition of divisibility:\n\n> By the definition of divisibility in $\\mathbb{Z}$, $12 \\mid 4(a-b)$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that\n> $4(a-b) = 12k$.\n\nNow, we can cancel out the factor of $4{:}$\n\n> Dividing this equation by $4$, we obtain\n> $a-b = 3k$,\n> which in turn implies\n> $3 \\mid (a-b)$.\n\nFinally, we use the definition of modular congruence again:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition of modular congruence, we have\n> $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod} \\: 3)$.\n> By proving the contrapositive, we proved the initial statement, as required."] [:db/cas 17592186157096 :question/worked-solution "Since $2$ and $7$ are coprime, then according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 7 = 14\n$$\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:2) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 1 + 2t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡5 & (mod7) \\\\ 1 + 2t & ≡5 & (mod7) \\\\ 2t & ≡4 & (mod7)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $7$ is a relatively small modulus, by trial and error, we have that $4 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:7)$. So, we multiply both sides by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2t & ≡4 & (mod7) \\\\ 4 \\cdot 2t & ≡16 & (mod7) \\\\ t & ≡2 & (mod7)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 2 + 7s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 + 2t \\\\\n&= 1 + 2(2 + 7s) \\\\\n&= 5 + 14s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 5 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:14)$ is the unique solution modulo $14$, and $x=5$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system." "Since $2$ and $7$ are coprime, then according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 7 = 14\n$$\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:2) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 1 + 2t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡5 & (\\bmod 7) \\\\ 1 + 2t & ≡5 & (\\bmod 7) \\\\ 2t & ≡4 & (\\bmod 7)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $7$ is a relatively small modulus, by trial and error, we have that $4 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:7)$. So, we multiply both sides by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2t & ≡4 & (\\bmod 7) \\\\ 4 \\cdot 2t & ≡16 & (\\bmod 7) \\\\ t & ≡2 & (\\bmod 7)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 2 + 7s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 + 2t \\\\\n&= 1 + 2(2 + 7s) \\\\\n&= 5 + 14s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 5 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:14)$ is the unique solution modulo $14$, and $x=5$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system."] [:db/cas 17592186157098 :question/worked-solution "Since $2$, $3$, and $5$ are coprime, then according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 = 30\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system in two steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the common solution for the first and second congruences of the system.\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:2) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 1 + 2t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡2 & (mod3) \\\\ 1 + 2t & ≡2 & (mod3) \\\\ 2t & ≡1 & (mod3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $3$ is a relatively small modulus, we can solve it by trial and error. We have that $2 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$. So, we multiply both sides by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2t & ≡1 & (mod3) \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2t & ≡2 & (mod3) \\\\ t & ≡2 & (mod3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 2 + 3s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get the common solution for the first and second congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 + 2t \\\\\n&= 1 + 2(2 + 3s) \\\\\n&= 5 + 6s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Find the common solution of all three congruences.\n\nNow, substituting the common solution of the first two congruences into the third congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡3 & (mod5) \\\\ 5 + 6s & ≡3 & (mod5) \\\\ 6s & ≡ - 2 & (mod5) \\\\ s & ≡3 & (mod5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\ns = 3 + 5u\n$$\n\nwhere $u \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for the common solution of the first two congruences, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 5 + 6s \\\\\n&= 5 + 6(3 + 5u) \\\\\n&= 23 + 30u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 23 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:30)$ is the unique solution modulo $30$, and $x=23$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system." "Since $2$, $3$, and $5$ are coprime, then according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 = 30\n$$\n\nWe will solve the system in two steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the common solution for the first and second congruences of the system.\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:2) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 1 + 2t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡2 & (\\bmod 3) \\\\ 1 + 2t & ≡2 & (\\bmod 3) \\\\ 2t & ≡1 & (\\bmod 3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $3$ is a relatively small modulus, we can solve it by trial and error. We have that $2 \\cdot 2 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:3)$. So, we multiply both sides by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2t & ≡1 & (\\bmod 3) \\\\ 2 \\cdot 2t & ≡2 & (\\bmod 3) \\\\ t & ≡2 & (\\bmod 3)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 2 + 3s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get the common solution for the first and second congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 1 + 2t \\\\\n&= 1 + 2(2 + 3s) \\\\\n&= 5 + 6s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Find the common solution of all three congruences.\n\nNow, substituting the common solution of the first two congruences into the third congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡3 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 5 + 6s & ≡3 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 6s & ≡ - 2 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ s & ≡3 & (\\bmod 5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\ns = 3 + 5u\n$$\n\nwhere $u \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for the common solution of the first two congruences, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 5 + 6s \\\\\n&= 5 + 6(3 + 5u) \\\\\n&= 23 + 30u\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 23 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:30)$ is the unique solution modulo $30$, and $x=23$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system."] [:db/cas 17592186157100 :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta$ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sin(7\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n7\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.$\n\n![](images/71/712d56708d2346f502a229c20596e7aa24ce4be13564e9461784c9114c55065a.png)\n\nSince there are $7$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =7\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\theta ))\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{4}(\\theta -\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta$ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\sin(7\\theta) = 0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $7\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n7\\theta = 0, \\pi \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad \\theta=0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{7}.$\n\n![](images/71/712d56708d2346f502a229c20596e7aa24ce4be13564e9461784c9114c55065a.png)\n\nSince there are $7$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =7\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(7\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /7}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14\\theta ))\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\frac{7}{4}(\\theta -\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14\\theta ))|}_{0}^{\\pi /7} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi ))-(0-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{7}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157102 :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\pi,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/76/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(-1,0).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $(x+1)^2+y^2=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = -2\\cos\\theta.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ r & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $(-1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\pi,$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/76/769c13bea2dfc91aa3a507df1b7a763ae0da7591e0d6ba5face12268efb88240.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: -\\sec\\theta \\leq r \\leq -2\\cos\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta }^{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157103 :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\!.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $$\n\\displaystyle x^2+\\left( y-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^2=\\dfrac14,\n$$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = \\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/12/1284a8b4b3f1634b4f7586d40ab8adf9cf93c69484b7ed069f138c8055e88892.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq \\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ centered at $\\left(0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\!.$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For $$\n\\displaystyle x^2+\\left( y-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)^2=\\dfrac14,\n$$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r = \\sin\\theta.$\n\n- For $y=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta.$ Our curves intersect at the point $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$ So, the entire region lies between the radial lines $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/12/1284a8b4b3f1634b4f7586d40ab8adf9cf93c69484b7ed069f138c8055e88892.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta)$ as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}\\csc\\theta \\leq r \\leq \\sin\\theta, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [r]_{1/2\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(2{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157104 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{r}{{r}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ represents a portion of the washer enclosed between the circles of radii $3$ and $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 3 \\leq r \\leq 4, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{r}{{r}^{2}({\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{3}^{4}\\frac{1}{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157175 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={\\tan }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157206 :question/worked-solution "If\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Po}\\left(\\lambda\\right)\n$$\n\nis a Poisson random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[X]} = \\sqrt{\\lambda}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Po}\\left(\\dfrac{20}{9}\\right)\\!\n$$\n\nwe have the following standard deviation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{Var[X]} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Po}\\left(\\lambda\\right)\n$$\n\nis a Poisson random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[X]} = \\sqrt{\\lambda}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Po}\\left(\\dfrac{20}{9}\\right)\\!\n$$\n\nwe have the following standard deviation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSD[X] &= \\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var} [X]} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{20}{9}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157210 :question/worked-solution "We interpret a \"success\" as the event that a $2$ is shown and a \"failure\" when some other number is displayed. So, the success probability is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ represent the number of rolls we must do until a 2 is obtained. So, we can model $X$ using a geometric distribution:\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nRecall that, if\n\n$$\nX \\sim\\textrm{Geom}\\left(p\\right)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{1-p}{p^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nwe have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= (1 - \\frac{1}{8})/(\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{1}{64}\\right)) \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We interpret a \"success\" as the event that a $2$ is shown and a \"failure\" when some other number is displayed. So, the success probability is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ represent the number of rolls we must do until a 2 is obtained. So, we can model $X$ using a geometric distribution:\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nRecall that, if\n\n$$\nX \\sim\\textrm{Geom}\\left(p\\right)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{1-p}{p^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nwe have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [X] &= (1 - \\frac{1}{8})/(\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{1}{64}\\right)) \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157214 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $49$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n49 =2^5 + 2^4 + 2^0 = 32+16+1\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n11^{49} = 11^{32 + 16 + 1} = 11^{32} \\cdot 11^{16} \\cdot 11^{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can take the corresponding values from the given table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$a$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$11^a \\: \\text{mod} \\: 73$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{11}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$48$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$41$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{16}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n11^{49}\\begin{vmatrix}≡11^{32} \\cdot 11^{16} \\cdot 11^{1} & (mod73) \\\\ ≡16 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 11 & (mod73)\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "First, we write $49$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n49 =2^5 + 2^4 + 2^0 = 32+16+1\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n11^{49} = 11^{32 + 16 + 1} = 11^{32} \\cdot 11^{16} \\cdot 11^{1}\n$$\n\nNow, we can take the corresponding values from the given table:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$a$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$11^a \\: \\text{mod} \\: 73$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{11}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$48$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$41$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{4}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[lightgray,2pt]{16}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n11^{49}\\begin{vmatrix}≡11^{32} \\cdot 11^{16} \\cdot 11^{1} & (\\bmod 73) \\\\ ≡16 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 11 & (\\bmod 73)\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186157216 :question/worked-solution "First, we write $22$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n22 = 2^4+2^2+2^1 = 16 + 4 + 2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n9^{22} = 9^{16 + 4 + 2} =9^{16} \\cdot 9^{4} \\cdot 9^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the exponents modulo $37$ using consecutive squaring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9^{2} & ≡81≡7 & (mod37) \\\\ 9^{4} & ≡7^{2}≡49≡12 & (mod37) \\\\ 9^{8} & ≡12^{2}≡144≡33 & (mod37) \\\\ 9^{16} & ≡33^{2}≡1089≡16 & (mod37) \\\\ 9^{32} & ≡16^{2}≡256≡34 & (mod37)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, by multiplying the obtained exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{22} & ≡{9}^{16}\\cdot {9}^{4}\\cdot {9}^{2} & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡16\\cdot 12\\cdot 7 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡1\\hspace{0.167em}344 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡36\\cdot 37+12 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡12 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we write $22$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n22 = 2^4+2^2+2^1 = 16 + 4 + 2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n9^{22} = 9^{16 + 4 + 2} =9^{16} \\cdot 9^{4} \\cdot 9^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the exponents modulo $37$ using consecutive squaring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9^{2} & ≡81≡7 & (\\bmod 37) \\\\ 9^{4} & ≡7^{2}≡49≡12 & (\\bmod 37) \\\\ 9^{8} & ≡12^{2}≡144≡33 & (\\bmod 37) \\\\ 9^{16} & ≡33^{2}≡1089≡16 & (\\bmod 37) \\\\ 9^{32} & ≡16^{2}≡256≡34 & (\\bmod 37)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, by multiplying the obtained exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{22} & ≡{9}^{16}\\cdot {9}^{4}\\cdot {9}^{2} & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡16\\cdot 12\\cdot 7 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡1\\hspace{0.167em}344 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡36\\cdot 37+12 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) \\\\  & ≡12 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}37) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157555 :question/problem "Given that $X∼Geom(0.12)$, what is $Var[X]$? Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." "Given that $X∼Geom(0.12)$, what is $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$? Round your answer to $1$ decimal place."] [:db/cas 17592186157564 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $12^{48}mod73$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following table containing some powers of $12$ modulo $73$.*\n\n| **$a$** | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$12^{a}mod73$** | $12$ | $71$ | $4$ | $16$ | $37$ | $55$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Which of the following is equivalent to $12^{48}\\bmod 73$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following table containing some powers of $12$ modulo $73$.*\n\n| **$a$** | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$12^{a}\\bmod 73$** | $12$ | $71$ | $4$ | $16$ | $37$ | $55$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186157643 :question/problem "Let $a$ and $b$ be integers. Prove that if $a + 3≢b + 3(mod8)$, then $a≢b(mod8)$.\n\nThe contrapositive of the given statement is\n$a≡b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one." "Let $a$ and $b$ be integers. Prove that if $a + 3≢b + 3(\\bmod 8)$, then $a≢b(\\bmod 8)$.\n\nThe contrapositive of the given statement is\n$a≡b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one."] [:db/cas 17592186157645 :question/problem "Let $a$ and $b$ be integers. Prove that $a≢b(mod7)$ if $5a≢5b(mod7)$.\n\nThe contrapositive of the given statement is\n$a≡b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one." "Let $a$ and $b$ be integers. Prove that $a≢b(\\bmod 7)$ if $5a≢5b(\\bmod 7)$.\n\nThe contrapositive of the given statement is\n$a≡b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one."] [:db/cas 17592186157646 :question/problem "Consider the following statement:\n\n> If $a - 7≢b - 7(mod13)$, then $a≢b(mod13)$.\n\nSuppose we wish to prove this statement using the contrapositive. To do this, we need to prove the following equivalent statement:\n\n$a≡b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" "Consider the following statement:\n\n> If $a - 7≢b - 7(\\bmod 13)$, then $a≢b(\\bmod 13)$.\n\nSuppose we wish to prove this statement using the contrapositive. To do this, we need to prove the following equivalent statement:\n\n$a≡b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186157647 :question/problem "Consider the following statement:\n\n> $a≢b(mod13)$ provided that $5a≢5b(mod13)$.\n\nSuppose we wish to prove this statement using the contrapositive. To do this, we need to prove the following equivalent statement:\n\n$5a≡5b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}" "Consider the following statement:\n\n> $a≢b(\\bmod 13)$ provided that $5a≢5b(\\bmod 13)$.\n\nSuppose we wish to prove this statement using the contrapositive. To do this, we need to prove the following equivalent statement:\n\n$5a≡5b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186157648 :question/problem "Let $a$ and $b$ be integers. Prove that $3a≢3b(mod15)$ if $a≢b(mod5)$.\n\nThe contrapositive of the given statement is\n$a≢b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}}\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one." "Let $a$ and $b$ be integers. Prove that $3a≢3b(\\bmod 15)$ if $a≢b(\\bmod 5)$.\n\nThe contrapositive of the given statement is\n$a≢b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}}\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one."] [:db/cas 17592186157660 :question/problem "Let $a$ and $b$ be integers. Prove that if $a - 3≢b - 3(mod5)$, then $a≢b(mod5)$.\n\nThe contrapositive of the given statement is\n$a≢b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one." "Let $a$ and $b$ be integers. Prove that if $a - 3≢b - 3(\\bmod 5)$, then $a≢b(\\bmod 5)$.\n\nThe contrapositive of the given statement is\n$a≢b$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nWe will prove this statement instead of the original one."] [:db/cas 17592186157682 :question/problem "Let $a ∈ Z$. Prove that if $5∣a$ and $7∣a$, then $35∣a$.\n\nSince $5$ and $7$ are coprime, their {{field-1}} is given by\n$lcm(5, 7)$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" "Let $a ∈ Z$. Prove that if $5∣a$ and $7∣a$, then $35∣a$.\n\nSince $5$ and $7$ are coprime, their {{field-1}} is given by\n$\\operatorname{lcm} (5, 7)$ {{field-2}} {{field-3}}"] [:db/cas 17592186157698 :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=\\begin{bmatrix}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}{J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) & =\\begin{bmatrix}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & {\\sec }^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{1}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡({J}_{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157702 :question/problem "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$a$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$3^{a}mod59$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$26$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUse the above table containing some powers of $3$ modulo $59$ to complete the following calculation of $3^{40}mod59$.\n\n$3^{32} \\cdot 3^{16}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}} {{field-5}} {{field-6}} {{field-7}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$a$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$32$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>$3^{a}\\bmod 59$</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$9$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$22$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$12$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$26$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$27$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUse the above table containing some powers of $3$ modulo $59$ to complete the following calculation of $3^{40}\\bmod 59$.\n\n$3^{32} \\cdot 3^{16}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}} {{field-5}} {{field-6}} {{field-7}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186157703 :question/problem "Complete the following calculation of $4^{27}mod37$.$4^{16} \\cdot 4^{8} \\cdot 4^{2} \\cdot 4^{1}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}} {{field-5}} {{field-6}} {{field-7}}\n\n*Hint:* You may wish to represent $27$ as a sum of powers of $2$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Complete the following calculation of $4^{27}\\bmod 37$.$4^{16} \\cdot 4^{8} \\cdot 4^{2} \\cdot 4^{1}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}} {{field-5}} {{field-6}} {{field-7}}\n\n*Hint:* You may wish to represent $27$ as a sum of powers of $2$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186157707 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|8-(-4)|,|4-4|,|-3-(-1)|) \\\\  & =max(12,0,2) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=\\max (|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\max (|8-(-4)|,|4-4|,|-3-(-1)|) \\\\  & =\\max (12,0,2) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157711 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|1-(-7)|,|-2-8|) \\\\  & =max(8,10) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=\\max (|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={\\left\\|\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\|}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\max (|1-(-7)|,|-2-8|) \\\\  & =\\max (8,10) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157723 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $15^{26}mod37$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following table containing some powers of $15$ modulo $37$.*\n\n| **$a$** | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$15^{a}mod37$** | $15$ | $3$ | $9$ | $7$ | $12$ | $33$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Which of the following is equivalent to $15^{26}\\bmod 37$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following table containing some powers of $15$ modulo $37$.*\n\n| **$a$** | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$15^{a}\\bmod 37$** | $15$ | $3$ | $9$ | $7$ | $12$ | $33$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186157724 :question/problem "| **$a$** | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$13^{a}mod83$** | $13$ | $3$ | $9$ | $81$ | $4$ | $16$ |\n\nUse the above table containing some powers of $13$ modulo $83$ to complete the following calculation of $13^{26}mod83$.\n\n$13^{1} \\cdot 13^{2} \\cdot 13^{4}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}} {{field-5}} {{field-6}} {{field-7}} {{field-8}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "| **$a$** | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $16$ | $32$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| **$13^{a}\\bmod 83$** | $13$ | $3$ | $9$ | $81$ | $4$ | $16$ |\n\nUse the above table containing some powers of $13$ modulo $83$ to complete the following calculation of $13^{26}\\bmod 83$.\n\n$13^{1} \\cdot 13^{2} \\cdot 13^{4}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}} {{field-5}} {{field-6}} {{field-7}} {{field-8}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186157725 :question/problem "Complete the following calculation of $7^{34}mod61$.$7^{32} \\cdot 7^{16} \\cdot 7^{4} \\cdot 7^{2}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}} {{field-5}} {{field-6}} {{field-7}}\n\n*Hint:* You may wish to represent $34$ as a sum of powers of $2$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "Complete the following calculation of $7^{34}\\bmod 61$.$7^{32} \\cdot 7^{16} \\cdot 7^{4} \\cdot 7^{2}$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}} {{field-4}} {{field-5}} {{field-6}} {{field-7}}\n\n*Hint:* You may wish to represent $34$ as a sum of powers of $2$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186157736 :question/worked-solution "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $8$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({8}^{2x}) & <{log}_{8}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ 2x & <{log}_{8}⁡({6}^{-1}) \\\\ 2x & <-{log}_{8}⁡(6) \\\\ x & <-\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{8}⁡(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {8}^{2x} & <\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the logarithm to base $8$ of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{8}⁡({8}^{2x}) & <{\\log }_{8}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right) \\\\ 2x & <{\\log }_{8}⁡({6}^{-1}) \\\\ 2x & <-{\\log }_{8}⁡(6) \\\\ x & <-\\frac{1}{2}{\\log }_{8}⁡(6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157761 :question/problem "Let $a ∈ Z$. Prove that if $3∣a$ and $8∣a$, then $24∣a$.\n\nSince $3$ and $8$ are coprime, we have $gcd(3, 8) =$\n{{field-1}}." "Let $a ∈ Z$. Prove that if $3∣a$ and $8∣a$, then $24∣a$.\n\nSince $3$ and $8$ are coprime, we have $\\gcd (3, 8) =$\n{{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186157792 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3} & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{3})=2{y}^{3}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{3})=6x{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{3} & 6x{y}^{2} \\\\ 6x{y}^{2} & 6{x}^{2}y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=2\\cdot 6-6\\cdot 6=-24.\n$$" "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=\\det ({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3} & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{3})=2{y}^{3}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6x{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{3})=6x{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{3} & 6x{y}^{2} \\\\ 6x{y}^{2} & 6{x}^{2}y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=2\\cdot 6-6\\cdot 6=-24.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157828 :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd3af53e4d4f02c5978e8b4bc8508fb0455a0a09e7222377df68a2297bc30dae.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" "![](images/cd/cd3af53e4d4f02c5978e8b4bc8508fb0455a0a09e7222377df68a2297bc30dae.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186157828 :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=4\\cdot A=8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=4\\cdot A=8{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157829 :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n![](images/22/220bd5e2559f7e3ef483b1315d1dd98771cf81d8d181a161b87d8084773259e6.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =4\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta $ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta $ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\n![](images/22/220bd5e2559f7e3ef483b1315d1dd98771cf81d8d181a161b87d8084773259e6.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =4\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{\\cos }^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157831 :question/problem "![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" "![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186157831 :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=3\\cdot A=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$" "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/09/09bc06001905956e32a4ff8cfff25a875a6933e19c365b2f9b159d8711a05048.png)\n\nTherefore, the total area bounded by the polar curve is \n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=3\\cdot A=\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /18}^{5\\pi /18}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157832 :question/problem "![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" "![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?"] [:db/cas 17592186157832 :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}]}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}]}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{11\\pi /6}{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$" "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/3a/3aa82e021a1871bd17abfd72570452bc6aefe358a8fddd3cb8e92bb6ff91c5a9.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{4\\pi }{3}]}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{4\\pi }{3},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}]}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{11\\pi /6}{\\sin }^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157834 :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/68/68f70968b0dd256159dbfdcbfde4f2f63578237db1770e019c8fb19a5a638eaa.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at $(0,1).$ \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}{\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta -2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1 & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta )-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1 \\\\ r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the limits for the parameter $\\theta .$ Our curves intersect at the points $(-1,1)$ and $(1,1).$ So, the entire region $D$ lies between the polar lines $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},$ as depicted below.  \n\n![](images/68/68f70968b0dd256159dbfdcbfde4f2f63578237db1770e019c8fb19a5a638eaa.png)\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\le r\\le 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{3\\pi /4}{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186157905 :question/problem "Solve the inequality $10{log}_{2}⁡(x-6)-3<2.$" "Solve the inequality $10{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-6)-3<2.$"] [:db/cas 17592186157905 :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{log}_{2}⁡(x-6)-3 & <2 \\\\ 10{log}_{2}⁡(x-6) & <5 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x-6) & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-6)} & <{2}^{1/2} \\\\ x-6 & <\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & <6+\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-6>0\\,⇒\\,x>6\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<6+\\sqrt{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,6+\\sqrt{2}).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>6,$ which can be expressed as $(6,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,6+\\sqrt{2})\\cap (6,\\infty )=(6,6+\\sqrt{2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (6,6+\\sqrt{2}),$ which can also be written as $6<x<6+\\sqrt{2}.$" "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-6)-3 & <2 \\\\ 10{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-6) & <5 \\\\ {\\log }_{2}⁡(x-6) & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-6)} & <{2}^{1/2} \\\\ x-6 & <\\sqrt{2} \\\\ x & <6+\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-6>0\\,⇒\\,x>6\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<6+\\sqrt{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,6+\\sqrt{2}).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>6,$ which can be expressed as $(6,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,6+\\sqrt{2})\\cap (6,\\infty )=(6,6+\\sqrt{2})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (6,6+\\sqrt{2}),$ which can also be written as $6<x<6+\\sqrt{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186157963 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={sin}^{2}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={sin}^{2}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is not of the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only." "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={\\sin }^{2}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sin }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2(-x)) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{4}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is not of the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-a}^{a}h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186157992 :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $E[X] = \\frac{3}{2}$, compute $Var[X]$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{ {x}^{4}}} , \\hspace{1em} & x \\ge  1 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{2em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $E[X] = \\frac{3}{2}$, compute $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{ {x}^{4}}} , \\hspace{1em} & x \\ge  1 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{2em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186157994 :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $E[X] = \\frac{3}{2}$, compute $Var[X]$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3 {x}^{2}}{8}} , \\hspace{1em} & 0 \\le  x \\le  2 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{2em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $E[X] = \\frac{3}{2}$, compute $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{3 {x}^{2}}{8}} , \\hspace{1em} & 0 \\le  x \\le  2 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{2em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186157995 :question/problem "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $E[X] = 3$, compute $Var[X]$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{9}} , & 0 < x < 3 , \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{9}} ( 6 - x ) , & 3 \\le  x < 6 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{2em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $E[X] = 3$, compute $\\operatorname{Var} [X]$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f ( x ) = \\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{9}} , & 0 < x < 3 , \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{9}} ( 6 - x ) , & 3 \\le  x < 6 , \\\\ 0 , \\hspace{2em} & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{array} \\right.\n$$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [:db/cas 17592186158483 :tutorial/content "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers $n_1,n_2,\\ldots,n_k$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(n_i,n_j)=1\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\ni \\neq j\n$$\n\nthen for any integers $a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_k$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}x \\equiv  {a}_{1} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{1} ) \\\\ x \\equiv  {a}_{2} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{2} ) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} \\vdots  &  \\\\ x \\equiv  {a}_{k} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{k} )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo $n_1 n_2 \\cdots n_k$.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the smallest natural number solution to the following system of linear congruences:\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}x \\equiv  4 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 ) \\\\ x \\equiv  7 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 9 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis system can be interpreted as follows. Suppose we have $x$ identical \"bricks\" and we know that:\n\n- if we stack them in piles with $5$ bricks each, then the last remaining pile will contain $4$ bricks.\n- if we stack them in piles with $9$ bricks each, then the last remaining pile will contain $7$ bricks.\n\nHow can we find the smallest possible number $x$?\n\n![](images/dd/dd89c8b56cd455b7aafa41cd8d305a7455246141851c49d26ba5cf955f87de7a.png)\n\nFirst, we note that $5$ and $9$ are coprime. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 9 = 45\n$$\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 4 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:5) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 4 + 5t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 4 + 5t & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 5t & ≡3 & (mod9)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $9$ is a relatively small modulus, we can find the multiplicative inverse of $5$ by trial and error. We find that $2 \\cdot 5 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:9)$. So, we multiply both sides of the above congruence by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5t & ≡3 & (mod9) \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5t & ≡2 \\cdot 3 & (mod9) \\\\ t & ≡6 & (mod9)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 6 + 9s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 4 + 5t \\\\\n&= 4 + 5(6 + 9s) \\\\\n&= 34 + 45s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 34 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:45)$ is the unique solution modulo $45$. The solution $x=34$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system, and all other solutions are obtained by adding or subtracting multiples of $45$ to this solution.\n\nFinally, let's check that this is indeed correct. Substituting $x=34$ into the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}34 & ≡4 & (mod5) \\\\ 34 - 6(5) & ≡4 & (mod5) \\\\ 4 & ≡4 & (mod5), ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=34$ into the second congruence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}34 & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 34 - 3(9) & ≡7 & (mod9) \\\\ 7 & ≡7 & (mod9).✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers $n_1,n_2,\\ldots,n_k$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(n_i,n_j)=1\n$$\n\nwhenever\n\n$$\ni \\neq j\n$$\n\nthen for any integers $a_1,a_2,\\ldots,a_k$ the system\n\n$$\n{\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}x \\equiv  {a}_{1} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{1} ) \\\\ x \\equiv  {a}_{2} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{2} ) \\\\ \\hspace{1em} \\vdots  &  \\\\ x \\equiv  {a}_{k} & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} {n}_{k} )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo $n_1 n_2 \\cdots n_k$.\n\nFor example, suppose that we want to find the smallest natural number solution to the following system of linear congruences:\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}x \\equiv  4 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 5 ) \\\\ x \\equiv  7 & ( \\text{mod} \\hspace{0.222em} 9 )\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThis system can be interpreted as follows. Suppose we have $x$ identical \"bricks\" and we know that:\n\n- if we stack them in piles with $5$ bricks each, then the last remaining pile will contain $4$ bricks.\n- if we stack them in piles with $9$ bricks each, then the last remaining pile will contain $7$ bricks.\n\nHow can we find the smallest possible number $x$?\n\n![](images/dd/dd89c8b56cd455b7aafa41cd8d305a7455246141851c49d26ba5cf955f87de7a.png)\n\nFirst, we note that $5$ and $9$ are coprime. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo\n\n$$\n5 \\cdot 9 = 45\n$$\n\nFrom the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\nx \\equiv 4 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:5) \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = 4 + 5t, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second congruence, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x & ≡7 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ 4 + 5t & ≡7 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ 5t & ≡3 & (\\bmod 9)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSince $9$ is a relatively small modulus, we can find the multiplicative inverse of $5$ by trial and error. We find that $2 \\cdot 5 \\equiv 1 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:9)$. So, we multiply both sides of the above congruence by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5t & ≡3 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ 2 \\cdot 5t & ≡2 \\cdot 3 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ t & ≡6 & (\\bmod 9)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nt = 6 + 9s\n$$\n\nwhere $s \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Substituting this into the expression for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 4 + 5t \\\\\n&= 4 + 5(6 + 9s) \\\\\n&= 34 + 45s\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x \\equiv 34 \\: (\\text{mod}\\:45)$ is the unique solution modulo $45$. The solution $x=34$ is the smallest natural number solution to the system, and all other solutions are obtained by adding or subtracting multiples of $45$ to this solution.\n\nFinally, let's check that this is indeed correct. Substituting $x=34$ into the first congruence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}34 & ≡4 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 34 - 6(5) & ≡4 & (\\bmod 5) \\\\ 4 & ≡4 & (\\bmod 5), ✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=34$ into the second congruence, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}34 & ≡7 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ 34 - 3(9) & ≡7 & (\\bmod 9) \\\\ 7 & ≡7 & (\\bmod 9).✓\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186158507 :tutorial/content "When we have an exponential function in an inequality and the base of the exponential is greater than $1,$ we can take the logarithm of both sides of the inequality.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n2^x < 3,\n$$\n\nwe can take the logarithm (base $2$) of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & <{log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & <{log}_{2}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason why we're allowed to take a logarithm of both sides of an inequality is that taking a logarithm preserves the order of numbers. That is to say, if $a < b,$ then $\\log_n (a) < \\log_n (b),$ provided that $n > 1$ and $a,b>0.$\n\nTo see this concretely, consider the following order of numbers:\n\n$$\n0.5 < 1 < 2 < 3\n$$\n\nIf we take the logarithm (base $2$) of all the numbers above, they stay in the same order:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-1}_{ \\log_2(0.5) } < \\underbrace{0}_{ \\log_2(1) } < \\underbrace{1}_{ \\log_2(2) } < \\underbrace{ \\,\\, 1.585 \\,\\, }_{ \\log_2(3) }\n$$" "When we have an exponential function in an inequality and the base of the exponential is greater than $1,$ we can take the logarithm of both sides of the inequality.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n2^x < 3,\n$$\n\nwe can take the logarithm (base $2$) of both sides and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\log }_{2}⁡({2}^{x}) & <{\\log }_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x & <{\\log }_{2}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason why we're allowed to take a logarithm of both sides of an inequality is that taking a logarithm preserves the order of numbers. That is to say, if $a < b,$ then $\\log_n (a) < \\log_n (b),$ provided that $n > 1$ and $a,b>0.$\n\nTo see this concretely, consider the following order of numbers:\n\n$$\n0.5 < 1 < 2 < 3\n$$\n\nIf we take the logarithm (base $2$) of all the numbers above, they stay in the same order:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{-1}_{ \\log_2(0.5) } < \\underbrace{0}_{ \\log_2(1) } < \\underbrace{1}_{ \\log_2(2) } < \\underbrace{ \\,\\, 1.585 \\,\\, }_{ \\log_2(3) }\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186158511 :tutorial/content "When we have a logarithm in an inequality with base $n > 1,$ we can use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $n.$\n\nHowever, there is a catch: when we solve the resulting inequality, we must remember to remove extraneous solutions. We can do this by keeping only the solutions that are in the domain of the original logarithm.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\log_2 (x-1) < 4,\n$$\n\nwe write each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x-1)} & <{2}^{4} \\\\ x-1 & <16 \\\\ x & <17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-1 > 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x > 1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x < 17,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty, 17).$ But the domain of the logarithm is $x > 1,$ which can be expressed as $(1, \\infty).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 17) \\cap (1, \\infty) = (1, 17)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in (1,17),$ which can also be written as $1 < x < 17.$" "When we have a logarithm in an inequality with base $n > 1,$ we can use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $n.$\n\nHowever, there is a catch: when we solve the resulting inequality, we must remember to remove extraneous solutions. We can do this by keeping only the solutions that are in the domain of the original logarithm.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\log_2 (x-1) < 4,\n$$\n\nwe write each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{\\log }_{2}⁡(x-1)} & <{2}^{4} \\\\ x-1 & <16 \\\\ x & <17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-1 > 0 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x > 1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x < 17,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty, 17).$ But the domain of the logarithm is $x > 1,$ which can be expressed as $(1, \\infty).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 17) \\cap (1, \\infty) = (1, 17)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x \\in (1,17),$ which can also be written as $1 < x < 17.$"] [:db/cas 17592186158532 :tutorial/content "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. The domain of the parameter $t$ is\n\n$$\nt\\in[-\\pi,\\pi],\\qquad t\\neq \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe branches corresponding to different values of $t$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f787b25a9ed78b01a3a4d7a8c88c43649662384fd55df2bb690449704dc7697.png)\n\nThis parametrization contains discontinuities at $t=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ One consequence of this is that the curve appears to \"jump\" between different quadrants at the singular points.\n\nFor the most part, we won't worry too much about the domain of $t.$ However, it does become important when we're parametrizing part of a hyperbola, which we'll deal with later.\n\n**Note:** To understand where this parametrization comes from, recall the trigonometric identity\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 t = \\sec^2 t.\n$$\n\nRearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sec^2 t - \\tan^2 t  =1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sec}^{2}⁡t & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {tan}^{2}⁡t & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,x=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. The domain of the parameter $t$ is\n\n$$\nt\\in[-\\pi,\\pi],\\qquad t\\neq \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe branches corresponding to different values of $t$ are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4f/4f787b25a9ed78b01a3a4d7a8c88c43649662384fd55df2bb690449704dc7697.png)\n\nThis parametrization contains discontinuities at $t=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ One consequence of this is that the curve appears to \"jump\" between different quadrants at the singular points.\n\nFor the most part, we won't worry too much about the domain of $t.$ However, it does become important when we're parametrizing part of a hyperbola, which we'll deal with later.\n\n**Note:** To understand where this parametrization comes from, recall the trigonometric identity\n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2 t = \\sec^2 t.\n$$\n\nRearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sec^2 t - \\tan^2 t  =1,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sec }^{2}⁡t & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,y=a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ {\\tan }^{2}⁡t & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,x=b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186158540 :tutorial/content "We've used the fact that the parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. Now, we'll demonstrate why this is true.\n\nFirst, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t & ⟹ &  & \\operatorname{tan}⁡t & =\\frac{x}{b}, \\\\ y & =a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t & ⟹ &  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡t & =\\frac{y}{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the secant-tangent identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{x}{b}\\right)}^{2}+1 & ={\\left(\\frac{y}{a}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin. Therefore, the parametric equations do indeed describe a vertical hyperbola." "We've used the fact that the parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin are\n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. Now, we'll demonstrate why this is true.\n\nFirst, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =b\\operatorname{tan}⁡t & ⟹ &  & \\operatorname{tan}⁡t & =\\frac{x}{b}, \\\\ y & =a\\operatorname{sec}⁡t & ⟹ &  & \\operatorname{sec}⁡t & =\\frac{y}{a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the secant-tangent identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\tan }^{2}⁡t+1 & ={\\sec }^{2}⁡t \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{x}{b}\\right)}^{2}+1 & ={\\left(\\frac{y}{a}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the Cartesian equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin. Therefore, the parametric equations do indeed describe a vertical hyperbola."] [:db/cas 17592186158552 :tutorial/content "The **Diffie-Hellman** shared secret exchange protocol is a **public key cryptographic algorithm** that allows two parties to share a secret message over an insecure communication channel. It uses modular exponentiation to derive the shared secret without directly transmitting it.\n\nThe discovery of the Diffie-Hellman protocol laid the foundations for many modern cryptographic protocols for securely transferring online information. Before considering the Diffie-Hellman algorithm in detail, let's review its mathematical foundations.\n\nSuppose that we need to compute $8^{24} \\:\\text{mod}\\: 59$. To do that efficiently, we proceed as follows:\n\nFirst, we write $24$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n24 = 2^4+2^3 = 16+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n8^{24} = 8^{16+8} = 8^{16} \\cdot 8^{8}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the exponents modulo $59$ using consecutive squaring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8^{2} & ≡64≡5 & (mod59) \\\\ 8^{4} & ≡5^{2}≡25 & (mod59) \\\\ 8^{8} & ≡25^{2}≡625≡35 & (mod59) \\\\ 8^{16} & ≡35^{2}≡1225≡45 & (mod59)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, by multiplying the highlighted exponents, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{24} & ≡{8}^{16}\\cdot {8}^{8} & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡45\\cdot 35 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡1\\hspace{0.167em}575 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡26\\cdot 59+41 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡41 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The **Diffie-Hellman** shared secret exchange protocol is a **public key cryptographic algorithm** that allows two parties to share a secret message over an insecure communication channel. It uses modular exponentiation to derive the shared secret without directly transmitting it.\n\nThe discovery of the Diffie-Hellman protocol laid the foundations for many modern cryptographic protocols for securely transferring online information. Before considering the Diffie-Hellman algorithm in detail, let's review its mathematical foundations.\n\nSuppose that we need to compute $8^{24} \\:\\text{mod}\\: 59$. To do that efficiently, we proceed as follows:\n\nFirst, we write $24$ as a sum of powers of $2{:}$\n\n$$\n24 = 2^4+2^3 = 16+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n8^{24} = 8^{16+8} = 8^{16} \\cdot 8^{8}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the exponents modulo $59$ using consecutive squaring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8^{2} & ≡64≡5 & (\\bmod 59) \\\\ 8^{4} & ≡5^{2}≡25 & (\\bmod 59) \\\\ 8^{8} & ≡25^{2}≡625≡35 & (\\bmod 59) \\\\ 8^{16} & ≡35^{2}≡1225≡45 & (\\bmod 59)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, by multiplying the highlighted exponents, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{24} & ≡{8}^{16}\\cdot {8}^{8} & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡45\\cdot 35 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡1\\hspace{0.167em}575 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡26\\cdot 59+41 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) \\\\  & ≡41 & \\hspace{0.222em}(\\text{mod}\\hspace{0.222em}59) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186158609 :tutorial/content "We'll now justify why the Jacobian determinant gives the (local) area scale factor of a transformation.\n\nConsider a rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane of length $\\delta u,$ width $\\delta v,$ whose bottom-left corner is at the point $(u_0,v_0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/9a/9a7d700c343fd2807e70b43d688c2f47ed3724e88fc9849e23aa20ee0be10b15.png)\n\nFurthermore, we assume the following:\n\n- $\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow \\mathbb R^2$ is a $C^1$ transformation throughout $\\Delta,$ and contains no critical points.\n\n- The image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the region $D.$\n\n- The image of $(u_0,v_0)$ is $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- The image of $S_1$ is $S_1',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $A.$\n\n- The image of $S_2$ is $S_2',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $B.$\n\nSince $S_1'$ is the image of $S_1,$ it can be parametrized by varying $u$ only. Similarly, since $S_2'$ is the image of $S_2,$ it can be parametrized by varying $v$ only. So, the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} + \\delta  u , {v}_{0} ) \\\\ y \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} + \\delta  u , {v}_{0} )\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\n\\qquad \n\n\\mathbf b\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} , {v}_{0} + \\delta  v ) \\\\ y \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} , {v}_{0} + \\delta  v )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\delta u$ is small, $S_1'$ can be approximated by a line segment. Similarly, since $\\delta v$ is small, $S_2'$ can also be approximated by a line segment. Let's construct two vectors $\\mathbf{s}_1$ and $\\mathbf{s}_2$ that span our two segments, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1ff10cdcde237698a28c6f76053de50745ef12c67dfc5290ac6e06893d201972.png)\n\nWe can find the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ by subtracting the position vectors of its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1}=a-\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that when $\\delta u$ is small, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{∂x}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u} \\\\ {\\frac{∂y}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf s_1 \\approx\n\\delta u \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  u}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  u}}\\end{array} \\right]}_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nUsing similar arguments, we have the following approximation for $\\mathbf s_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf s_2 \\approx\n\\delta v \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  v}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  v}}\\end{array} \\right]}_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nThe area of $D$ is approximately equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)\n\\approx\n\\left|\n\\det {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} | & | \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{s}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{s}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} | & | \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em}\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\right|.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our approximations for $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2,$ we arrive at the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ≈{\\left|det\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\,\\,\\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\right|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & ={|\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot (\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\frac{∂y}{∂u})|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & =\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot {|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll now justify why the Jacobian determinant gives the (local) area scale factor of a transformation.\n\nConsider a rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane of length $\\delta u,$ width $\\delta v,$ whose bottom-left corner is at the point $(u_0,v_0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/9a/9a7d700c343fd2807e70b43d688c2f47ed3724e88fc9849e23aa20ee0be10b15.png)\n\nFurthermore, we assume the following:\n\n- $\\mathbf T: \\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow \\mathbb R^2$ is a $C^1$ transformation throughout $\\Delta,$ and contains no critical points.\n\n- The image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the region $D.$\n\n- The image of $(u_0,v_0)$ is $(x_0,y_0).$\n\n- The image of $S_1$ is $S_1',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $A.$\n\n- The image of $S_2$ is $S_2',$ and its endpoints are $(x_0,y_0)$ and $B.$\n\nSince $S_1'$ is the image of $S_1,$ it can be parametrized by varying $u$ only. Similarly, since $S_2'$ is the image of $S_2,$ it can be parametrized by varying $v$ only. So, the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} + \\delta  u , {v}_{0} ) \\\\ y \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} + \\delta  u , {v}_{0} )\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\n\\qquad \n\n\\mathbf b\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} , {v}_{0} + \\delta  v ) \\\\ y \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} , {v}_{0} + \\delta  v )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\delta u$ is small, $S_1'$ can be approximated by a line segment. Similarly, since $\\delta v$ is small, $S_2'$ can also be approximated by a line segment. Let's construct two vectors $\\mathbf{s}_1$ and $\\mathbf{s}_2$ that span our two segments, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1ff10cdcde237698a28c6f76053de50745ef12c67dfc5290ac6e06893d201972.png)\n\nWe can find the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ by subtracting the position vectors of its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1}=a-\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that when $\\delta u$ is small, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{∂x}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u} \\\\ {\\frac{∂y}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf s_1 \\approx\n\\delta u \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  u}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  u}}\\end{array} \\right]}_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nUsing similar arguments, we have the following approximation for $\\mathbf s_2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf s_2 \\approx\n\\delta v \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  v}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  v}}\\end{array} \\right]}_{(u_0,v_0)}\n$$\n\nThe area of $D$ is approximately equal to the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)\n\\approx\n\\left|\n\\det {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}\\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} | & | \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} {\\mathbf{s}}_{1} & {\\mathbf{s}}_{2} \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} \\\\ \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em} | & | \\hspace{0.222em} \\hspace{0.222em}\\end{array} \\right]} \n\\right|.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our approximations for $\\mathbf s_1$ and $\\mathbf s_2,$ we arrive at the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ≈{\\left|\\det \\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\,\\,\\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\delta u\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\delta v\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\,\\,\\end{bmatrix}\\right|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & ={|\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot (\\frac{∂x}{∂u}\\frac{∂y}{∂v}-\\frac{∂x}{∂v}\\frac{∂y}{∂u})|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} \\\\  & =\\delta u\\delta v\\cdot {|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186158610 :tutorial/content "Suppose we encrypt the $3$-letter English word $\\text{DOG}$ to ciphertext using the RSA cipher with the public key\n\n$$\n(e,n)=(13,51)\n$$\n\nWe'll assume that we use the usual English alphabet (shown above) during the encryption, where each letter is encrypted separately. Let's determine the ciphertext that results from the encryption.\n\n*Hint: Use the `mod` function on your calculator or a software package to compute the required powers modulo $n$.*\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">A</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">B</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">C</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">D</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">E</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">F</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">G</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">H</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">I</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">J</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">K</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">L</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\" data-cell-weight=\"bold\">M</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n