 is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\operatorname{arcoth}  x) = \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{8}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=8\\int \\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=8\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166610 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{2x^2+1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=\n$$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\pi}{\\sqrt{2x^2+1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166610 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2+1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arsinh u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $2x^2 = \\left(\\sqrt{2}\\,x\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u =\\sqrt{2}\\,x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,x)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{\\arsinh}u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{\\arsinh}(\\sqrt{2}\\,x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2+1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\operatorname{arsinh}  u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $2x^2 = \\left(\\sqrt{2}\\,x\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u =\\sqrt{2}\\,x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\sqrt{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,x)}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{\\arsinh}u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\text{\\arsinh}(\\sqrt{2}\\,x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166611 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}} \\,\\textrm{d}x   =\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}} \\,\\textrm{d}x   =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166611 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nLet's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{3}{2} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\arcosh u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac x 2.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x =2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =3\\text{\\arcosh}u+C \\\\  & =3\\text{\\arcosh}\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nLet's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{\\sqrt{x^2-4}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{3}{2} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = \\operatorname{arcosh}  u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac x 2.$ Differentiating, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x =2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =3\\text{\\arcosh}u+C \\\\  & =3\\text{\\arcosh}\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166612 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{10}{1 - 4x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x   =\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{10}{1 - 4x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x   =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166612 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u =\\artanh u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = \\left(2x\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{1-4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\text{\\artanh}u+C \\\\  & =5\\text{\\artanh}2x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $\\left|2x \\right| <    1.$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-u^2} \\, \\textrm{d}u =\\operatorname{artanh}  u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = \\left(2x\\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{10}{1-4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =5\\text{\\artanh}u+C \\\\  & =5\\text{\\artanh}2x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $\\left|2x \\right| <    1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186166613 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166613 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u = \\arcosh{u}+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac {x}{2} \\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac {x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $2\\sqrt{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\cdot (2 \\, \\textrm{d}u) \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\arcosh u \\Big|_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\arcosh{\\sqrt 5} - \\arcosh{2} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&=  \\ln\\left(\\sqrt 5 + \\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt 5\\right)^2 - 1}\\right) - \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right)^2 - 1}\\right)  \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{5}\\right) -  \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)  \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2 + \\sqrt{5}}{2 + \\sqrt{3}}  \\right)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2-1}}\\, \\textrm{d}u = \\operatorname{arcosh} {u}+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $\\left(\\dfrac {x}{2} \\right)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u = \\dfrac {x}{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 2\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $2\\sqrt{5}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\sqrt{5}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 - 4}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int_{4}^{2\\sqrt{5}} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac {x}{2} \\right)^2 - 1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\cdot (2 \\, \\textrm{d}u) \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\int_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\sqrt{u^2 - 1}} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\operatorname{arcosh}  u \\Big|_{2}^{\\sqrt 5} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\operatorname{arcosh} {\\sqrt 5} - \\operatorname{arcosh} {2} \\\\[5pt]\n\n&=  \\ln\\left(\\sqrt 5 + \\sqrt{\\left(\\sqrt 5\\right)^2 - 1}\\right) - \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{\\left(2\\right)^2 - 1}\\right)  \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{5}\\right) -  \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{3}\\right)  \\\\[5pt]\n\n& = \\ln\\left(\\dfrac{2 + \\sqrt{5}}{2 + \\sqrt{3}}  \\right)\n\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166614 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\mid 3x \\mid \\sqrt{1+9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x =\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\mid 3x \\mid \\sqrt{1+9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166614 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosecant,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\mid u \\mid \\sqrt{1+u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = -\\arcsch u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n9x^2 = (3x)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u =3x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{1+(3x)^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse hyperbolic cosecant,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\mid u \\mid \\sqrt{1+u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = -\\operatorname{arcsch}  u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n9x^2 = (3x)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u =3x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{1+9{x}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|3x|\\sqrt{1+(3x)^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166615 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{4-x^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\frac{x}{2}\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic secant,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = -\\arsech u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac x 2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =2\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}dx &= \\frac{1}{4}\\int(1)/(\\frac{x}{2}\\sqrt{1 - \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int\\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\cdot 2du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{du}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}arsech u + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}arsech \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{4-x^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\frac{x}{2}\\sqrt{1-\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic secant,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\,\\textrm{d}u = -\\operatorname{arsech}  u+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac x 2\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =2\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2}}}dx &= \\frac{1}{4}\\int(1)/(\\frac{x}{2}\\sqrt{1 - \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\int\\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\cdot 2du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\int\\frac{du}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}arsech u + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}  \\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166615 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{4-x^2}} \\,\\textrm{d}x =\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{4-x^2}} \\,\\textrm{d}x =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166616 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5}{1 - 4x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x =\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5}{1 - 4x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x =\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166616 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cotangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-u^2}\\, \\textrm{d}u =\\arcoth u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n4x^2 = \\left(2x\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{5}{1 - 4x^{2}}dx &= 5\\int(1)/(1 - (2x)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 5\\int\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}arcoth u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}arcoth 2x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition\n\n$$\n\\left|2x \\right| > 1\n$$" "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic cotangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1-u^2}\\, \\textrm{d}u =\\operatorname{arcoth}  u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n4x^2 = \\left(2x\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{5}{1 - 4x^{2}}dx &= 5\\int(1)/(1 - (2x)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= 5\\int\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}\\int\\frac{1}{1 - u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}arcoth u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{2}arcoth 2x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition\n\n$$\n\\left|2x \\right| > 1\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166617 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-9x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nEvaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-9x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166617 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x = 3\\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{3x\\sqrt{1-9x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic secant,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-u^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}u =-\\arsech u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n9x^2 = \\left(3x\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1 - 9x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= 3 \\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{3x\\sqrt{1 - \\left(3x\\right)^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= 3 \\int_{1/3}^{1/2} \\dfrac{1}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^2}}\\, \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &= \\int_{1/3}^{1/2} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{u \\sqrt{1-u^2}} \\\\[5pt] & = \\left.- \\arsech{u}\\right|_{1/3}^{1/2} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\left(\\arsech\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) - \\arsech\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\arsech\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) - \\arsech\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) \\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nNote that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x = 3\\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{3x\\sqrt{1-9x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for the inverse hyperbolic secant,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-u^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}u =-\\operatorname{arsech}  u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n9x^2 = \\left(3x\\right)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 3 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{9}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{6}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{3}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{1}{2}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{x\\sqrt{1 - 9x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= 3 \\int_{1/9}^{1/6} \\dfrac{1}{3x\\sqrt{1 - \\left(3x\\right)^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= 3 \\int_{1/3}^{1/2} \\dfrac{1}{u\\sqrt{1 - u^2}}\\, \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{3}\\,\\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &= \\int_{1/3}^{1/2} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{u \\sqrt{1-u^2}} \\\\[5pt] & = \\left.- \\operatorname{arsech} {u}\\right|_{1/3}^{1/2} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\left(\\operatorname{arsech} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) - \\operatorname{arsech} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) \\right) \\\\[5pt] &= \\operatorname{arsech} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) - \\operatorname{arsech} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166619 :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4x + 3 &= x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 2x + 2^{2} - 2^{2} + 3 \\\\\n&= (x - 2)^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x^2-4x+3}} \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt {(x-2)^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x-2$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 4x + 3}}dx &= \\int(2)/(\\sqrt{(x - 2)^{2} - 1})dx \\\\\n&= 2\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1}}du \\\\\n&= 2arcosh u + C \\\\\n&= 2arcosh (x - 2) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4x + 3 &= x^{2} - 2 \\cdot 2x + 2^{2} - 2^{2} + 3 \\\\\n&= (x - 2)^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{x^2-4x+3}} \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt {(x-2)^2-1}} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x-2$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 4x + 3}}dx &= \\int(2)/(\\sqrt{(x - 2)^{2} - 1})dx \\\\\n&= 2\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1}}du \\\\\n&= 2arcosh u + C \\\\\n&= 2\\operatorname{arcosh}  (x - 2) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166622 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{1} \\sinh {\\left(2x+1\\right)} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{1} \\sinh {\\left(2x+1\\right)} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166623 :question/worked-solution "Let\n\n$$\nu=3x+1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intcsch (3x + 1)\\coth (3x + 1)dx &= \\intcsch u\\coth u \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\intcsch u\\coth udu \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(-csch u) + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3}csch (3x + 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let\n\n$$\nu=3x+1\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x =\\dfrac{1}{3} \\, \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\intcsch (3x + 1)\\coth (3x + 1)dx &= \\intcsch u\\coth u \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\intcsch u\\coth udu \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}(-csch u) + C \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csch}  (3x + 1) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166623 :question/problem "Calculate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\csch(3x+1)\\coth(3x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{csch} (3x+1)\\coth(3x+1) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166624 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} {\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2} x^2\\sinh{x^3}}\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" "Evaluate the integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} {\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{2} x^2\\sinh{x^3}}\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166624 :question/worked-solution "Let $u=x^3$. Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d} u = x^2\\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline x & - 2 & 2 \\\\ \\hline u & - 8 & 8 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\int_{-2}^{2} x^2\\sinh{x^3}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n&=\\int_{-2}^{2} \\sinh{x^3}\\cdot x^2\\,\\textrm{d}x\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{-8}^{8} \\sinh{u}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{d}u\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cosh u\\Big|_{-8}^{8}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cosh{8} - \\cosh{(-8)}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cosh{8} - \\cosh{8}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cdot 0\\\\[5pt]\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $u=x^3$. Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3x^2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{3}\\textrm{d} u = x^2\\, \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|}\\hline x & - 2 & 2 \\\\ \\hline u & - 8 & 8 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\int_{-2}^{2} x^2\\sinh{x^3}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n&=\\int_{-2}^{2} \\sinh{x^3}\\cdot x^2\\,\\textrm{d}x\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{-8}^{8} \\sinh{u}\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{3}\\, \\textrm{d}u\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cosh u\\Big|_{-8}^{8}\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cosh{8} - \\cosh{(-8)}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\cosh{8} - \\cosh{8}\\right)\\\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cdot 0\\\\[5pt]\n&=0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166625 :question/problem "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x\\sinh{x^2}}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" "Calculate\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x\\sinh{x^2}}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166625 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nu= \\cosh{x^2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\\sinh{x^2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm{d}u = x\\sinh{x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x\\sinh{x^2}}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\cdot x\\sinh{x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\int \\dfrac{1}{u}\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm d u \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int \\dfrac{1}{u}\\, \\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid u \\mid +C \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\cosh{x^2}\\right)+ C \\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nu= \\cosh{x^2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2x\\sinh{x^2} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm{d}u = x\\sinh{x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and compute it, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} \\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{x\\sinh{x^2}}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\, \\textrm{d}x &= \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh{x^2}}\\cdot x\\sinh{x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x \\\\[5pt] &= \\int \\dfrac{1}{u}\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} \\, \\textrm d u \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\int \\dfrac{1}{u}\\, \\textrm{d}u \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln \\mid u \\mid +C \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\cosh{x^2}\\right)+ C \\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166629 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\coth{x}\\sinh{x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}$\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\coth{x}\\sinh{x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166629 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin{x}}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\coth{x}=\\dfrac{\\cosh{x}}{\\sinh{x}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-(-1)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2e-2{e}^{-1}}{2} \\\\  & =e-{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{\\cos{x}}{\\sin{x}}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\coth{x}=\\dfrac{\\cosh{x}}{\\sinh{x}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-(-1)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{1}-{e}^{-1}-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2e-2{e}^{-1}}{2} \\\\  & =e-{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166630 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$First, we write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x}\\textrm{d}x =\n\t\t\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\cdot \\tanh^2{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\tan{x}=\\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is  \n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{\\sinh{x}}{\\cosh{x}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {tanh}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}{{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{sech}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{e-{e}^{-1}}{e+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{{e}^{-1}+e}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}-\\frac{1-{e}^{2}}{1+{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$First, we write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x}\\textrm{d}x =\n\t\t\\dfrac{1}{2}\\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\cdot \\tanh^2{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNow we need to find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\tan{x}=\\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is  \n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{\\sinh{x}}{\\cosh{x}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the above identity to write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot {\\tanh }^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}{{\\sinh }^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{\\cosh }^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\operatorname{sech} }^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{tanh}⁡(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{e-{e}^{-1}}{e+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{{e}^{-1}-e}{{e}^{-1}+e}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}-\\frac{1-{e}^{2}}{1+{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{e}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166630 :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$" "$\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\tanh}{\\mathop{\\rm tanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int _{-1}^{1} \\dfrac{\\tanh^2{x}}{2\\sinh^2 x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166631 :question/problem "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\ln 3} \\tanh x   \\, \\textrm{d}x  }.\n$$" "Evaluate \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\ln 3} \\tanh x   \\, \\textrm{d}x  }.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166631 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\tan{x} = \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{\\sinh{x}}{\\cosh{x}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u = \\cosh{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sinh{x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\textrm{d}u = \\sinh{x} \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration according to the rule $u = \\cosh{x}.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\ln 3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5/3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\operatorname{sech} }{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{csch} }{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsinh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcosh} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{artanh} }{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arsech} }{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcsch} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\operatorname{arcoth} }{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\tan{x} = \\dfrac{\\sin{x}}{\\cos{x}}\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{\\sinh{x}}{\\cosh{x}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u = \\cosh{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sinh{x} \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\textrm{d}u = \\sinh{x} \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration according to the rule $u = \\cosh{x}.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\ln 3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$u$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{5}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{5/3}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1}^{5/3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186166633 :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the Pythagorean identity \n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x + \\sin^2 x= 1.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the hyperbolic Pythagorean identity\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2x -\\sinh^2x=1\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n1+\\sinh^2x=\\cosh^2x.\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we have\n\n$$\n\\sech x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{sinh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\right)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {sech}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C\\end{a